[2026-07-30 18:25] fix(e2e): three specs called the API through Playwright's `request` fixture after logging in on `page` — why: `request` is a SEPARATE context with its own empty cookie jar, so every call arrived with no session and was refused 401 before the behaviour under test ran. The 404 in grand-quiz's "a student cannot read another student's attempt" and the 400/404 in learn's step-id validation were unreachable, so both tests were permanently red while appearing to check authorization. Now use page.request.
[2026-07-30 18:25] fix(e2e): derive the ui-shell sidebar link counts from NAV_LINKS instead of hardcoding 5 and 7 — why: it had been failing since five nav links were added in 13c15a1. The same derivation was applied to nav-links.test.tsx at integration for exactly this reason and this spec was missed; a hand-kept count drifts, and the drift is invisible until someone reads the failure.
[2026-07-30 18:25] docs: add docs/SUBJECT_SECTIONS.md — the section model, how to release a subject, what runtime-editable would cost, and an appendix recording the latency measurements so the next person does not re-derive them.
[2026-07-30 18:25] test(e2e): TODO warnings on four interactive-learning specs and one interactive-exercises spec that fail against the current UI — why: they are NOT fixture problems (learn content is seeded: 18 modules, 104 steps) but a mismatch between each stream's specs and its own rendered markup. Flagged rather than skipped: a green skip would hide that "a lab actually running" and prefers-reduced-motion are currently unverified anywhere.

[2026-07-30 18:45] fix(videos): connect the three severed links between the curated video library and lecture pages — why: the owner reported no videos on lectures. Diagnosis: the render path was correct and starved of data at three independent points, each fatal alone. (a) `lectures.topic_key` was declared and migrated but NEVER written by anything, null on all 12 rows; (b) `topic_videos` held 0 rows because the harvester had never been run; (c) TopicVideoSection — packaged by the video-ingestion stream as a documented one-line drop-in, because that stream could not edit another stream's page — was imported by no page at all, so resolveLectureVideo was never called from a student route. The lecture page still called VideoEmbed with lecture.youtubeUrl, which is null on every seeded row, so "Video coming soon" was the only reachable branch.
[2026-07-30 18:45] feat(videos): add topicKey to the 12 seeded lectures, verified against curated-videos.json before writing — why: the topic slugs were authored to match the lecture titles but nothing had ever asserted that. The annotation script fails rather than writes if any key is absent from the curated file, so a typo cannot silently produce a lecture that joins to nothing.
[2026-07-30 18:45] fix(videos): backfill topic_key for already-seeded lectures instead of skipping them — why: the seeder's "exists, skip" rule would have left all 12 rows null forever, so every lecture would keep showing the placeholder no matter how many videos an admin approved. Guarded on `== null` so it is idempotent and never overwrites a key set by hand, and it touches ONLY that column: silently refreshing lecture text on a re-run is how an instructor's corrections disappear.
[2026-07-30 18:45] feat(videos): count a mapped topic towards `hasVideo` on week/lecture lists — why: the badge read "no video" for every lecture. Deliberately optimistic for the topic case; confirming an APPROVED row would cost a query per lecture on a list page, and the lecture page still resolves the real answer and still shows the honest placeholder when nothing is approved.
[2026-07-30 18:45] chore(videos): run the harvester over the 77 curated ids — 77/77 re-validated live through YouTube's keyless oEmbed endpoint (video exists AND permits embedding), 77 candidates inserted, 0 rejected. NOTHING is student-visible: every row lands status='candidate' and requires an admin approval at /admin/videos, which is the audit barrier the ingestion stream was built around.

[2026-07-30 19:05] test(e2e): invert weeks.spec.ts to assert an approved topic video PLAYS, discharging its own TODO — why: the only video assertion in the whole suite REQUIRED the "Video coming soon" placeholder, so all three severed links that produced the owner's bug were invisible to CI and would have stayed invisible. Asserts the src SHAPE (nocookie host + 11-char id) rather than a specific id, because which approved candidate wins is an admin's decision and must be free to change.

[2026-07-31 10:30] feat(exercises): persist editor drafts per exercise in localStorage (src/lib/exercises/persistence.ts, new) — why: the owner reported the live editor "does not keep the changes". There was NO persistence of any kind, anywhere: every edit lived only in Sandpack's in-memory state, so a reload, a nav away, or a crash discarded the student's work silently. Drafts are keyed by exercise id and stamped with a fingerprint of the STARTER files, so a draft written against content an instructor has since edited is discarded rather than restored against a changed brief. Size- and age-capped with a prune pass, because localStorage is a ~5 MB per-origin budget shared with the session and quota errors surface as an unrelated feature breaking.
[2026-07-31 10:30] fix(exercises): stop the live editor resetting itself — memoise the `files` object and the `options` object on the exercise IDENTITY, and React.memo the component on `exercise.id` — why: Sandpack's `useFiles` effect is keyed on `props.files` BY REFERENCE (@codesandbox/sandpack-react dist/index.mjs, deps `[props.files, props.customSetup, props.template]`); when that identity changes it rebuilds the file map from the prop, discarding edits, and recomputes activeFile from options.activeFile, which parse.ts pins to /index.html. Every producer of a SandpackExercise mints a fresh object per render, so ANY re-render above the editor would have wiped the student's work and snapped the tab back to HTML. SCOPE HONESTLY STATED: this is a latent hazard, not a proven reproduction — see the test note below.
[2026-07-31 10:30] fix(exercises): initMode "user-visible" -> "lazy" on the Sandpack provider — why: both defer the first mount until the exercise scrolls into view, which is what a lecture page carrying several iframes needs, but "user-visible" ALSO calls unregisterAllClients() when the element scrolls back OUT of view, tearing down the preview of an exercise the student is still working on. Scroll down to re-read the problem, scroll back, and the preview is blank until it re-initialises.
[2026-07-31 10:30] fix(exercises): reset-to-starter no longer calls sandpack.resetAllFiles() — why: that call restores the `files` PROP, and the editor now seeds that prop FROM the saved draft, so it would have "reset" the exercise to the student's own edits and appeared to do nothing. Reset now clears the draft and remounts the provider, which reads the starter because no draft remains.
[2026-07-31 10:30] fix(a11y): restore focus to the reset button after a reset — why: the remount that implements reset destroys the very button the student just activated. Invisible with a mouse; with a keyboard, focus falls to <body> and the next Tab restarts from the top of the document. Guarded on the button actually having had focus, so a mouse click does not raise an unrequested focus ring. Found by a component test failing on a detached node, not by inspection.
[2026-07-31 10:30] chore(exercises): write the fingerprint delimiters as \u0000/\u0001 escapes rather than literal control bytes — why: a raw NUL in the source made git and ripgrep classify persistence.ts as BINARY, so the file was silently invisible to every content search over the repo. Identical at runtime.
[2026-07-31 10:30] test(exercises): 4 e2e regression tests for tab state and drafts, plus 8 component tests and 21 unit tests — VERIFIED AGAINST THE PRE-FIX CODE, and the result is only partly what was wanted. Reverting LiveEditor.tsx makes the DRAFT test fail (proving the persistence half) but leaves the three TAB-STATE tests GREEN — i.e. the reported "switches back to HTML" symptom does not reproduce at /practice on either viewport. Both pages that host the editor are server components, so nothing above it re-renders and the by-reference hazard has no live trigger today. The tab tests are therefore guards against a real hazard, NOT proof the owner's symptom is fixed. TODO: reproduce the symptom with the owner (which page, which browser, what was clicked) before claiming that half is resolved.

[2026-07-31 11:05] perf(nav): add a loading boundary to both authenticated route groups — src/app/(app)/loading.tsx and src/app/(staff)/loading.tsx, the FIRST loading.tsx files in the repo — why: measured, the pages are not slow, the wait is invisible. Every page in both groups is `dynamic = "force-dynamic"` over Neon and costs 260-1002 ms of round trips (scripts/perf-roundtrips.ts: /weeks 260 ms depth 1, /dashboard 354 ms, /weeks/:id 519 ms, lecture 512 ms; scripts/perf-probe.ts: /leaderboard 514 ms overall and 993 ms per-week, /problems 1002 ms — all close to the 240 ms warm round-trip floor recorded in docs/SUBJECT_SECTIONS.md, so 25fe2d2 already took the DB work about as far as it goes without cross-request caching). With no loading boundary anywhere in src/app, the router had NOTHING to render for that interval and held the previous page on screen, unchanged and unmarked, until the RSC payload arrived. The second effect is larger than the first: Next's default Link prefetch for a dynamic route fetches only the shell down to the nearest loading boundary, so with no boundary the sidebar's prefetches were warming nothing reusable. Placed beside each group's layout.tsx, NOT at src/app/, so the boundary wraps `children` only and the top bar and sidebar stay mounted across a navigation.
[2026-07-31 11:05] feat(nav): PageSkeleton picks its shape from the destination pathname (src/lib/navigation/loading-shape.ts, new) — why: loading.tsx is handed no props at all, so it cannot tell /leaderboard from /weeks, and a generic grey block replaced by a table is a SECOND layout change — the user sees two transitions instead of one, which reads worse than none. A client component reading usePathname (already in the bundle for the sidebar's active state, and already committed to the destination URL at click time) selects from one prefix table, scanned longest-prefix-first like src/middleware.ts, instead of twenty per-segment loading.tsx files each drifting from a page owned by a different stream. A unit test derives its coverage from NAV_LINKS so a new nav row cannot silently fall through to the prose fallback.
[2026-07-31 11:05] feat(nav): NavLinkItem gives the clicked sidebar row a spinner via useLinkStatus (Next 15.3+; this repo runs 15.5.22) — why: the row the user's eye is on changed in no way for the entire 260-1002 ms. The hook flips in the same tick as the click, so the acknowledgement lands in ~16 ms at 60 Hz rather than after a round trip, and it is the only honest indicator for the case a skeleton cannot cover — a navigation whose prefetch has not completed. Extracted out of Sidebar's inline .map() because useLinkStatus returns { pending: false } forever unless it is called from a DESCENDANT of its Link. Every data-testid, data-active and aria-current is unchanged, so the existing ui-shell specs address the same nodes.
[2026-07-31 11:05] chore(nav): left Link prefetch at its default rather than prefetch={true} — why: a full prefetch of a dynamic route makes the server RENDER it, i.e. run its Neon queries, for every link in the viewport. The sidebar shows up to nine at once, so each page load would fan out into nine speculative page renders against a pool sized max: 5 (src/db/index.ts). The default fetches the shell down to the boundary added above, which is the cheap half and the half that removes the blank wait. No auth exposure either way: NAV_LINKS is already role-filtered, src/middleware.ts still gates the prefetch request itself at the edge, and the shell carries no student data.
[2026-07-31 11:05] chore(nav): SKIPPED React/Next View Transitions for this item, deliberately — why: it is still behind `experimental.viewTransition` in 15.5.22 and exposed as `unstable_ViewTransition`, so adopting it means turning on an experimental flag repo-wide for a cross-fade. The measured defect was 260-1002 ms of nothing happening; a transition animates the moment the content SWAPS, which is the one moment that was never the problem. Worth revisiting once the API is stable. Streaming Suspense boundaries inside individual pages were also skipped, for an ownership reason rather than a technical one: those pages belong to other streams, and the route-level boundary added here already streams the shell first.
[2026-07-31 11:05] a11y(nav): both new indicators degrade under prefers-reduced-motion instead of disappearing — motion-safe:animate-pulse on the skeleton and motion-safe:animate-spin on the row spinner, which compile to a `prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference` query — why: the rule this repo already follows in src/lib/exercises/reduced-motion.ts is that reduced motion degrades the presentation and never removes the information. The skeleton keeps its full layout as flat blocks and the spinner ring keeps its transparent quadrant so it still reads as a spinner when still. Expressed as Tailwind variants rather than tokens so this change does not touch globals.css, which another stream owns. The skeleton also carries role="status" + aria-busy + a visually hidden "Loading…" — grey rectangles announce nothing, and the state they replaced announced nothing either.
[2026-07-31 11:05] test(nav): 19 new vitest tests (8 unit on the shape table, 6 on PageSkeleton, 5 on NavLinkItem) — RUN AND GREEN: 53 passed across src/components/nav, src/lib/navigation and tests/unit/cross-stream-contracts.test.ts, plus `npx tsc --noEmit` clean. Also updated the next/link mock in nav-links.test.tsx to export useLinkStatus, without which every Sidebar render in that file would have thrown on a missing function rather than on anything to do with the nav map.
[2026-07-31 11:05] test(e2e): tests/e2e/ui-shell/navigation.spec.ts — 8 specs, WRITTEN BUT NOT RUN (six agents share port 3000 and one seeded database; the coordinator runs the suite serially afterwards). They throttle the RSC navigation response with page.route — the prefetch is deliberately left at full speed — so the pending window is deterministic rather than racing a fast local network. Read-only: they log in and navigate, and write nothing. TODO(coordinator): run these; the claim that pending state and the skeleton are ever OBSERVED is unproven until they pass, since vitest can only show the markup each state produces, not that the state occurs.
[2026-07-31 11:05] perf(nav): First Load JS unchanged — 115 kB on /dashboard, /weeks, /leaderboard and /practice, 138 kB on the staff routes, 102 kB shared, middleware 46.5 kB, IDENTICAL before and after — why the number is trustworthy: the working tree holds five other agents' in-flight edits and would not build at all (an unrelated missing export in src/lib/queue), so both builds were run in a throwaway git worktree at HEAD with only these files copied in. The shared chunk hash did not even change. Guarded because the repo has a documented history here — LazyExerciseList.tsx exists because a static import took a route from ~115 kB to 377 kB.

[2026-07-31 11:20] feat(ui): scope item 6 "Animations" to the shared primitives and their state changes, after auditing what the app actually has â€” why: the item arrived with no detail, so the first job was to establish the gap rather than guess. Audit result: the ONLY real animation in the product is the exercises stream's concept explainer (Framer Motion + src/lib/exercises/reduced-motion.ts, already complete and left untouched). Everything else was four unnamed magic numbers on the primitives â€” transition-colors duration-150 (Button.tsx:41), transition-shadow duration-150 (Card.tsx:40), transition-[width] duration-300 (ProgressBar.tsx:94), transition-transform duration-150 (StarRating.tsx:184) â€” three of which were near-invisible in practice and one of which never fired at all. So the work is polish on the primitives every page composes, not a new animation system.
[2026-07-31 11:20] feat(ui): motion scale in src/lib/motion/tokens.ts (new) â€” MOTION_MS fast 150 / base 250 / slow 400 / ambient 1200, all milliseconds â€” why: durations that exist only as inline Tailwind numbers cannot be reviewed for consistency and cannot be tested at all. Naming them makes it visible that a hover and a press are the same speed on purpose. The module RE-EXPORTS prefersReducedMotion/usePrefersReducedMotion from src/lib/exercises/reduced-motion.ts rather than reimplementing them: one predicate, one set of tests, two consumers.
[2026-07-31 11:20] perf(ui): every animation added here is a CSS keyframe or transition in globals.css â€” 0 kB of added JavaScript â€” why: framer-motion IS already a dependency but is confined behind the dynamic import in LazyExerciseList.tsx, which exists because a static import took a route from ~115 kB to 377 kB of First Load JS. The ui primitives are imported by essentially every route including /login, so importing framer-motion from Button or ProgressBar would put that weight on every page in the app in order to move a progress bar. CSS also runs before React mounts, which is exactly the point for a loading skeleton.
[2026-07-31 11:20] feat(ui): progress bars grow from zero on mount (@keyframes ui-progress-fill, 400 ms) â€” why: the existing transition-[width] was dead code on every real page. The inline width is server-rendered at its final value and nothing in the app mutates percent after mount, so the dashboard and week bars were simply PAINTED at 62% and never animated. aria-valuenow carries the final value from the first render, so assistive tech is never told 0% while the fill sweeps. Opt-out per call site (animateFill={false}) for lists and tables, where a dozen bars sweeping at once is a wave rather than polish.
[2026-07-31 11:20] feat(ui): interactive Cards lift 2 px on hover and return on press; Buttons, links styled with buttonClasses, and star targets scale to 98% while pressed â€” why: Card's shadow-sm -> shadow-md step is nearly invisible on a white panel over the #f4f4f6 surface, so week and lecture cards â€” which are whole-card links â€” had almost no hover affordance. The press response lives in BASE_CLASSES rather than in the <button> branch so a navigation <a> built from buttonClasses feels identical to the button beside it.
[2026-07-31 11:20] fix(ui): move Button's colour transition out of Tailwind's transition-colors and into the .ui-press rule â€” why: transition is winner-takes-all, one rule owns the whole property list, and globals.css is UNLAYERED while Tailwind utilities live in @layer utilities â€” so an unlayered "transition: transform ..." beats transition-colors outright and would have silently deleted the hover colour fade on every button in the app. Verified against the compiled stylesheet (postcss + @tailwindcss/postcss over globals.css): the ui-* rules emit outside the @layer theme/base/components/utilities cascade. One rule now lists colour AND transform at the same 150 ms.
[2026-07-31 11:20] feat(ui): StarRating previews the rating under the pointer or keyboard focus before it is committed â€” why: grading is the interaction this control exists for and it was blind â€” the only way to see what a 4 looked like was to award a 4. The preview is purely visual: it never moves data-value, never moves aria-checked and never calls onChange, so a mouse crossing the widget on its way elsewhere cannot regrade a submission. It previews DOWNWARD too (5 -> 2), which is the correction case a max()-based preview gets wrong, and it fires on focus as well as hover so it is not a mouse-only feature.
[2026-07-31 11:20] feat(ui): new Skeleton primitive with a shimmer sweep, exported from @/components/ui â€” why: there was no placeholder component in the barrel and no loading.tsx anywhere under src/app, so every route arrived with the previous page still on screen. This is the one animation in the stream where the MOVEMENT CARRIES INFORMATION: a static grey block is indistinguishable from a layout bug. Under reduced motion the sweep stops and the gradient flattens, so that meaning is carried by role="status" + aria-busy, which are present in both modes. NOT yet wired into any route â€” src/app/(app)/** belongs to other streams and to a concurrent page-transition change. Stated rather than half-done.
[2026-07-31 11:20] fix(a11y): reduced motion now switches the TRANSFORMS off, not merely their duration â€” why: the blanket rule in globals.css clamps everything to 1 ms, which is the right floor for a colour fade but not for a hover lift: a hover:-translate-y-0.5 utility still MOVES the card, it just moves it in 1 ms. A user asking for reduced motion is asking for the element not to jump under their pointer, and only a real rule can say "transform: none" in the reduce branch â€” which is why these are hand-written classes rather than utilities on the JSX. Every keyframe added here is entry-only with no fill-mode, so "animation: none" is a complete override: nothing is left half-drawn and no content waits on a keyframe to become readable.
[2026-07-31 11:20] test(ui): 30 new vitest cases (motion tokens 16, Skeleton 7, Card 4, Toast 3) plus 6 StarRating preview cases and 3 ProgressBar cases â€” RAN: "npx vitest run src/lib/motion/tokens.test.ts src/components/ui" => 66/66 green, and "npx tsc --noEmit" clean for every file this change touches (the 19 remaining lines of tsc output are pre-existing failures in problems/, submissions/ and queue/, none of them touched here). tokens.test.ts reads globals.css OFF DISK and fails if a duration there disagrees with MOTION_MS â€” the same drift guard the branding colours already needed.
[2026-07-31 11:20] test(e2e): tests/e2e/ui-shell/animations.spec.ts asserts COMPUTED styles in both media modes, using page.emulateMedia({ reducedMotion }) copied from the interactive-exercises spec â€” why: jsdom has no cascade, no @media evaluation and no animation data in getComputedStyle, so a component test passes identically whether globals.css animates a class, animates it wrongly, or never defines it at all. NOT RUN â€” the harness binds port 3000 and mutates one shared seeded database while other agents are working, so e2e is the parent's serial step. Every assertion in that file is therefore UNVERIFIED; the vitest and postcss checks above cover the class wiring and the stylesheet, not the browser's computed result.
[2026-07-31 11:20] chore(ui): deliberately NOT animated â€” badge/lock state changes. A week unlocking is the obvious candidate and it does not work: the unlock happens across a navigation (quiz submit -> redirect), so there is no before-and-after in one DOM to animate, and a pop on mount would fire on every page load for every already-unlocked week, which is theatre rather than feedback. Revisit if a lock state ever changes client-side. Separately, a coordination TODO is recorded in Skeleton.tsx: the concurrent navigation work added PageSkeleton.tsx with its own motion-safe:animate-pulse bars because it (correctly) would not edit globals.css, which this stream owns; it should compose the Skeleton primitive once both changes are on the branch, or the product ships two different loading shimmers.

