[2026-07-31 11:45] feat(queue): async job queue with database-level idempotency, bounded retries and a dead-letter state (src/lib/queue/**, new) — why: ESTABLISHED BY READING FIRST, and three of the four candidates were REJECTED (recorded in full at the top of src/lib/queue/types.ts). Sheet ingestion already has a scheduled sweep AND a unique index on (assignment_id, sheet_row_ref) — a queue in front of it is a scheduler in front of a scheduler. Leaderboard rebuilds run in one transaction under pg_advisory_xact_lock over tens of rows; deferring them would make the board lag a grade for a whole drain interval to save nothing. Video harvesting is an operator script behind a human approval gate, not request-triggered. The one real case is OUTBOUND EMAIL: it is external I/O bounded by three 10_000 ms timeouts (SMTP_TIMEOUT_MS, src/lib/mail/smtp.ts:44), it is worth retrying, and it must not double-send. Password-reset mail is deliberately NOT queued — it is the one message a human is actively waiting on, and delaying it to the next drain would make the reset flow feel broken.
[2026-07-31 11:45] feat(queue): the "your assignment has been graded" email — the app had NO grade notification of any kind — wired as one real producer and one real consumer so the queue is demonstrably used, not scaffolding. Producer: src/lib/instructor/grading.ts#applyGrade, chosen over gradeSubmission because that function is ALSO reached by recordGrade and by the ingestion path, and a re-ingest touching graded_at would email a student about a grade they were told about days ago. applyGrade is the single adapter both the instructor UI and the instructor route go through, which is exactly the scope the notification means. Consumer: src/lib/queue/handlers/submission-graded-email.ts.
[2026-07-31 11:45] feat(db): `jobs` table with a UNIQUE INDEX on idempotency_key (src/db/schema.ts) — why: this index IS the item, not a performance nicety. A select-then-insert check cannot resolve the race: under READ COMMITTED two concurrent serverless invocations both see no row and both insert. `enqueueJob` inserts with ON CONFLICT (idempotency_key) DO NOTHING and reports `created` from whether the INSERT returned a row — i.e. Postgres deciding, not application code guessing. Single-column deliberately, because ON CONFLICT needs one constraint to name as its arbiter, so the job kind is folded into the key string instead. No foreign key to the entity a job is about: cascading a submission delete into the queue would silently drop the notification, whereas a job whose target vanished should dead-letter with a readable reason. NOTE: migration 0003_flashy_forgotten_one.sql was generated by a CONCURRENT agent's db:generate run, which picked up this table along with their own course_access_requests; it is correct and already applied. Do NOT generate a second migration for `jobs` — re-running db:generate now reports an empty diff, verified.
[2026-07-31 11:45] feat(queue): idempotency key scoped to (submission, graded_at), not to the submission alone (src/lib/queue/keys.ts) — why: the two obvious alternatives both fail, and the failure is silent in both directions. Keying on the submission alone means an instructor who corrects a 3-star rating to 5 leaves the student holding a notification about a grade that no longer exists, with no second mail ever coming. Stamping the key with Date.now() at enqueue makes every enqueue unique, so the unique index never fires and there is no idempotency at all. graded_at is written inside the grading transaction, so a double-clicked Save shares one timestamp and collides, while a genuine regrade writes a new one and correctly notifies again.
[2026-07-31 11:45] feat(queue): buildIdempotencyKey REFUSES an over-long key instead of hashing it — why: hashing is the obvious way to keep keys short and it is the wrong trade here. A hash collision does not corrupt data, it makes the second enqueue look like a duplicate and SILENTLY DISCARDS THE JOB, with nobody ever finding out. Throwing puts the mistake at the producer where it is visible. The charset is also restricted to identifier characters, so a caller cannot key on editable text — a student name changes when it is edited, producing a different key for the same logical job, which is a duplicate email.
[2026-07-31 11:45] feat(queue): retries bounded at 5 attempts with exponential backoff in MILLISECONDS — 30_000 / 60_000 / 120_000 / 240_000 ms, plus or minus 20 percent jitter, ceiling 3_600_000 ms (src/lib/queue/policy.ts) — why: roughly 7.5 minutes of retrying is sized against the failure this exists to survive, a relay briefly refusing (an app-password rate limit, a restart). Anything still failing after seven minutes is a configuration fault and belongs on the dead-letter list where an operator can see it, not papered over by a queue that retries for a day. The jitter is not decoration: when the ONE relay refuses, every queued mail job fails in the same tick, computes the same deterministic delay, and retries in lockstep against the relay that just rate-limited it.
[2026-07-31 11:45] feat(queue): terminal `dead` status plus GET/POST /api/admin/jobs (new route) — why: the requirement is that a job failing forever becomes VISIBLE, not merely that it stops. A status column alone satisfies that only for somebody holding a psql prompt. The route exposes counts, the dead list, and a requeue action so the state is actionable; requeue demands an explicit id list or kind, because "resend every dead email in the system" must not be something an empty POST can do by accident. An unrecognised ?status= filter returns nothing rather than everything, so a typo cannot make an operator conclude there are no dead jobs. Dead rows are never swept by purgeSucceededJobs — a dead letter that disappears on a timer is exactly the silent vanishing being prevented.
[2026-07-31 11:45] feat(queue): claim with FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED, attempts incremented AT CLAIM, and a 120_000 ms lease (src/lib/queue/store.ts) — why: three separate bugs, three clauses. Without SKIP LOCKED two overlapping drains (a cron tick landing while an after()-triggered drain is still running — routine on Vercel) both select the same rows and the student gets the email twice. Incrementing attempts at COMPLETION instead of at claim would make a job that kills its worker immortal, because the count never moves. Without the lease-reclaim branch, a serverless function terminated between claiming and completing leaves the row `running` forever and the mail is silently never sent. completeJob also matches on `attempts`, so a stalled worker cannot overwrite the result written by the worker that stole its job — which would otherwise resurrect a succeeded job and send the email a second time.
[2026-07-31 11:45] feat(queue): drain triggers are after() on the request plus an authenticated /api/cron/drain-jobs — NO setInterval, and no vercel.json entry — why: BE PLAIN ABOUT VERCEL. A serverless function is frozen when its response is flushed; a setInterval worker ticks happily in `next dev` and processes NOTHING in production, which is the worst available failure because it only shows up where nobody is watching. So the primary trigger is next/server's after(), which runs post-response inside the same invocation: the instructor waits zero extra milliseconds and the email goes out seconds later. The scheduled floor is the cron route. It is NOT added to vercel.json, and not only because that file is another stream's: FREE_STACK.md commits this project to Vercel HOBBY, whose documented limits are two cron jobs invoked once per DAY, and vercel.json has already spent both slots on ingest-submissions and finalize-exams — a once-a-day drain would be worse than none, because it would look like a working scheduler. FREE_STACK.md already names free GitHub Actions cron as this project's scheduler for exactly this reason; a six-line workflow curling the route with the CRON_SECRET every five minutes is all that is needed, and it is not added here because .github/workflows belongs to devops-testing. WHAT THIS COSTS, stated rather than implied: without that workflow the queue still works for the common path, but a retry scheduled 240 s out waits for the next request that triggers a drain. That is a property of the platform, not of this code.
[2026-07-31 11:45] feat(mail): renderSubmissionGradedMail and previewFeedback (src/lib/mail/templates.ts) — why: this template renders the first genuinely UNTRUSTED strings to reach mail markup in this app — instructor feedback (up to 4000 chars per gradeSubmissionSchema) and the assignment title. escapeHtml is load bearing here rather than defensive, unlike the reset template whose only interpolations are a config string and a hex token. The subject additionally strips CR/LF, which escaping does not help with: a header value is not markup, and an embedded CRLF is a header-injection vector an instructor can paste into a title. Feedback is capped at 400 chars because the email is a notification, not a second copy of a record the instructor can still edit.
[2026-07-31 11:45] feat(queue): the handler re-reads its target instead of trusting a payload snapshot, and drops itself when graded_at has moved — why: a job can sit through four retries over minutes, and an instructor can regrade inside that window; a snapshot payload would cheerfully email a score that no longer exists and the student would have no way to tell which figure is real. The supersede check reports SUCCEEDED, not failure — a regrade already enqueued its own job under a different key, so nothing went wrong and dead-lettering it would put a non-problem on the operator's list. The tolerance is 1000 ms rather than exact equality because Postgres keeps microseconds and a JS Date keeps milliseconds; a strict check would classify every notification as superseded and the queue would look perfectly healthy while sending nothing.
[2026-07-31 11:45] test(queue): 57 vitest unit tests across policy (18), keys (15), drain (20) and registry (4), plus 14 for the handler and 12 for the mail template — ALL RUN, ALL GREEN (npx vitest run src/lib/queue src/lib/mail: 6 files, 92 tests passed, including the 21 pre-existing mail tests). The drain suite runs a job through all five attempts to `dead` with an injected in-memory store, so the backoff is asserted by reading the numbers it returns rather than by sleeping — a timing-based version would be flaky on CI and deleted within a month. It also pins the off-by-one that decides whether a job gets four retries or five, the "handler ran but the result could not be recorded" hole (logged loudly; the one case this design cannot make idempotent by itself), and the lease-reclaim path. STATED HONESTLY: no unit test proves FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED or the unique index — those are Postgres behaviours and a fake store only MODELS them.
