[2026-07-31 13:40] fix(queue): `jobs.run_after` and the lease are now written by POSTGRES's clock (`now()` / `now() + make_interval`), never by `new Date()` â€” why: THIS IS THE CAUSE OF THE FAILING E2E, not a tidy-up. Eligibility is decided in SQL by `run_after <= now()`, so a value produced by the Node process's clock was being compared against a different clock and the difference was being treated as a schedule. Measured on this machine: the app clock runs ~1_080 ms AHEAD of the Neon instance's, which makes a freshly enqueued job ineligible for about a second. Irrelevant for a 30_000 ms backoff and fatal for the zero-delay case, because the zero-delay case is drained by an `after()` callback that fires a few hundred ms after the enqueue: the drain claimed nothing, returned an empty report, and with no scheduled drain the row sat at `queued`/`attempts=0` for good. The evidence was on the row itself â€” job 6 in the shared database had `run_after` 823 ms LATER than the `created_at` the database stamped on the same INSERT. `completeJob` re-derives the retry time the same way from `next.delayMs`, so `NextState.runAfter` stays the pure policy's answer that policy.test.ts asserts while the app clock is out of the eligibility path entirely.
[2026-07-31 13:40] feat(queue): .github/workflows/drain-jobs.yml â€” a GitHub Actions cron on `*/5 * * * *` calling POST /api/cron/drain-jobs with the CRON_SECRET bearer token, and NO vercel.json entry â€” why: the queue had no scheduled drain at all, so the retry ladder (30/60/120/240 s) was arithmetic that nobody executed: a retry scheduled 240_000 ms out waited for the next request that happened to trigger a drain, which at 03:00 is not a floor. It is not a third `crons` entry because FREE_STACK.md commits this project to Vercel Hobby, whose documented limits are TWO crons per project at DAILY granularity whatever expression is written, and both slots are spent on ingest-submissions and finalize-exams â€” a once-a-day drain would be worse than none because it would LOOK like a scheduler. FREE_STACK.md already names GitHub Actions cron as this project's scheduler. Requires two repository secrets, APP_ORIGIN and CRON_SECRET, both documented in the workflow header with where to obtain them; a missing one fails its own first step with a named error rather than curling an empty URL. FAILS LOUDLY: any non-200 exits 1 with an ::error naming what each status means (401 rotated secret, 403 the confused-deputy guard, 503 CRON_SECRET unset on the deployment), because the route deliberately answers 200 even when it dead-letters, so a non-200 is never "a bad day" â€” it is "nothing is draining". Three curl attempts 5000 ms apart, retried ONLY on the absence of any HTTP response, because retrying a 401 would convert a configuration fault into a slow green run. Dead letters raise a ::warning and a step summary, never a failure: a permanently red schedule is a schedule people stop reading. GitHub's own limits (best-effort timing, auto-disable after 60 days of repo inactivity, default-branch-only) are written up in the header rather than assumed away.
[2026-07-31 13:40] fix(mail): the double-send hole is CLOSED with a dispatch ledger â€” `mail_dispatches`, a new table with a UNIQUE INDEX on `dedupe_key`, plus src/lib/mail/dispatch.ts â€” why: the previous stream documented this rather than fixing it, and a loud log is not a fix. The sequence: a drain claims a job, the handler runs, the relay accepts the message, `completeJob` fails (one round trip on a free pooled endpoint, on a platform that kills functions at a wall-clock limit), the lease expires, the next drain correctly reclaims the row because that is what leases are for, and the student gets the email twice. The fix moves the decision IN FRONT of the side effect: `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (dedupe_key) DO NOTHING RETURNING id` decides who owns a send, before any I/O. The asymmetry is the whole point â€” a PRE-send write that fails means nothing was sent, so retrying is completely safe, whereas the POST-send window shrinks from "handler + policy + completeJob + the network under all of it" to one UPDATE issued immediately after the transport returned. `dedupeKey` IS the job's `idempotency_key`, one string for both guarantees so they cannot drift. Second layer, free: the Message-ID handed to the transport is SHA-256(dedupeKey) truncated to 128 bits, so a resend carries a byte-identical header that a receiving MTA or client MAY suppress â€” hashed rather than embedded because a Message-ID travels to the student and the key contains a submission id and the exact millisecond a grade was written.
[2026-07-31 13:40] fix(mail): the residual risk is NAMED, BOUNDED and NOT called exactly-once â€” why: this is at-least-once plus deduplication, and exactly-once across a process boundary and an SMTP relay is not purchasable, because the relay cannot enlist in a Postgres transaction. The three states of (`sent_at`, `failed_at`) are the entire protocol: (set,*) never send again; (null,set) a DEFINITE failure, so an ordinary retry; (null,null) a sender claimed the key and never came back and it is UNKNOWN whether the message left. For that last state INDETERMINATE_RESEND_LIMIT = 2 resends exactly ONCE and then refuses, and the handler dead-letters the refusal with "It is NOT known whether the student received this email". Resend-once rather than never because the overwhelming majority of the wall clock between claim and record is the SMTP call itself (up to ~30_000 ms against three 10_000 ms timeouts), so a disappearance more likely means the send did not complete; and a student who never hears has to notice an absence, while a student who hears twice can see both copies. Bounded at once rather than retried because an unbounded resend on an unknown state is how one flapping relay becomes eight copies of one email. `listInFlightDispatches` makes the unknown state queryable, so it is an operator's item rather than a silent second email on every lease expiry.
[2026-07-31 13:40] test(queue): src/lib/queue/store.integration.test.ts â€” 12 tests against the REAL Postgres, RUN AND GREEN, proving the two guarantees no unit test could â€” why: drain.test.ts MODELS `for update skip locked` and MODELS the unique index, so it agrees with itself by construction â€” drop `jobs_idempotency_key_idx` or delete the words `skip locked` and all 40-odd of its tests still pass while production sends duplicate email. That was flagged as a known gap. SKIP LOCKED is proved by holding an explicit claiming transaction OPEN on one raw connection while a second connection claims under a 4_000 ms `statement_timeout`: a plain `for update` BLOCKS on those locks, so the regression surfaces as a named timeout rather than a wrong value, and the second claim asks for TWO rows and must get exactly ONE. VERIFIED BY BREAKING IT: with `skip locked` removed from the statement the test fails at 5017 ms with a Postgres timeout; restored, it passes. Uniqueness is proved twice over â€” eight simultaneous enqueues of one key on eight connections yield exactly one row with exactly one `created: true`, AND `pg_indexes` is read directly to assert the index is `CREATE UNIQUE INDEX`, which catches a migration that recreated it as a plain index even if a race happened to serialise. Also proved: lease reclaim increments `attempts` (so a job that kills its worker is not immortal), the run_after clock fix in both directions, and four of the ledger's branches including two workers racing for one dedupe key. Coexists with five other agents on the shared database via a `__qint:` key prefix deleted in afterAll, a `kind` no handler routes, and `claimJobs({ onlyIds })` so foreign traffic cannot change what a claim returns.
[2026-07-31 13:40] fix(e2e): three specs in tests/e2e/queue/queue.spec.ts were SKIPPING silently, and the skip was hiding the coverage that mattered â€” why: they guard on `process.env.CRON_SECRET`, playwright.config.ts loads no dotenv, and those three are the ONLY coverage of the authorised drain path â€” the endpoint a scheduler now calls every five minutes. So the arrangement was the worst available: never exercised locally, only ever in CI (ci.yml sets CRON_SECRET in the job env). Fixed with `import "dotenv/config"` in the spec rather than by editing playwright.config.ts, which is another stream's file; dotenv never overwrites a variable that is already set, so CI is unaffected. The remaining guard now carries a message saying to set the variable rather than accept the skip. The "never stuck" spec additionally asserts `run_after <= created_at` BEFORE it polls, so the clock regression reports its own cause instead of a mysterious "still queued", and its terminal assertion now explains what `attempts=0` versus `attempts>0` means to whoever reads the failure. NOT RUN â€” six agents, one port, one shared database; the coordinator runs e2e serially.
[2026-07-31 13:40] test(queue): src/lib/queue/schedule.test.ts asserts PROPERTIES OF A YAML FILE from a unit test â€” why: the availability gap was not a bug in any function, it was an ABSENT FILE, and nothing in TypeScript notices that a workflow has been deleted, renamed, quietly switched to hourly, or edited to swallow a non-200. Each of those silently restores the exact gap this change closes, because the common path keeps working via `after()`. So the test reads the workflow and asserts the schedule exists and is sub-hourly (`*/N` in the minute field â€” a plain `0` is hourly, longer than the whole retry ladder), that a non-200 exits 1, that both secrets are checked, that dead letters are a warning and not a failure, and that a running drain is never cancelled mid-batch. Properties, not text: reformatting the workflow does not break it, removing its guarantees does. One assertion had to be narrowed from the word "cookie" to the curl FLAGS, because the workflow legitimately contains that word in the prose explaining a 403 â€” it was failing against its own documentation.
[2026-07-31 13:40] feat(contracts): the three async-queues routes are now in ROUTES and ROUTE_AUTH â€” POST /api/cron/drain-jobs as "cron", GET and POST /api/admin/jobs as "admin" â€” why: the coordinator's call, not the implementing stream's. Enforcement does not change and never did: `requireCron` reads CRON_SECRET directly and `apiGuard` takes its level as an argument, so both handlers were already guarded exactly as these rows describe. What changes is that the map stops omitting three live routes, which is the only way its `Record<RouteKey, RouteAuth>` exhaustiveness check can catch a FUTURE route that forgot to authorize itself â€” a contract that lies about which routes exist cannot do the one job it exists for. The drain is "cron" rather than "admin" deliberately, and stricter: a drain sends mail to students, so an admin session that could trigger it would be a way to mail the whole cohort from a browser.
