[2026-07-31 15:10] fix(submissions): ingestion now names the wrong-publish-setting failure instead of blaming the Form's wording — why: "File -> Share -> Publish to web" defaults to "Web page", and choosing CSV is a second dropdown that is easy to miss. A sheet published that way answers 200 OK with a full HTML document, so fetchPublishedCsv accepted it, Papa Parse read the markup as a one-column CSV, mapColumns found no email column, and the run aborted `no_email_column` — which sends the operator to inspect their Form's question text, the one thing that is not wrong. New `html_not_csv` abort reason carrying the actual fix in prose. Detected at BOTH layers: content-type in fetch-csv.ts, plus a body sniff of the leading 512 characters, because Google's publish endpoint has been observed answering text/plain and any proxy can rewrite the header — a header-only check would be defeated by the commonest variant of the failure it exists to catch. Only the LEADING bytes are sniffed so that a student answering "anything else you want us to know?" with "<html>" cannot take the whole cohort's ingest down; that false positive is asserted against explicitly. `looksLikeHtml` lives in csv.ts, not fetch-csv.ts, so that module keeps the property its header claims — no import that touches the network — and fetch-csv imports it rather than the reverse.
[2026-07-31 15:10] feat(submissions): every abort reason now carries WHAT TO DO, in `ABORT_ADVICE` (types.ts) and `IngestReport.abortDetail` — why: a reason code is for aggregation and a human is not an aggregator. `html_not_csv` alone leaves the operator needing to know that it means "change a dropdown in the publish dialog", which is knowledge that lives in this repository and nowhere in their head. One table, so the log line, the API response and the report page cannot give different advice. Never contains the sheet URL — that is a capability token (fetch-csv.ts).
[2026-07-31 15:10] test(submissions): 23 new cases for the real-world sheet shapes, 55/55 green in csv.test.ts and 14/14 in fetch-csv.test.ts — why: everything the pipeline had ever been tested against was a CSV this repository shaped itself, so the shapes a REAL published Google Sheet produces were entirely unverified. Now covered, each with the requirement that the outcome is reported and non-fatal rather than a crash or a silent drop: header question text edited past the exact alias (the substring pass catches it; the honest limit is asserted too — an edit that loses "email" aborts `no_email_column` rather than silently reporting zero responses), columns reordered (irrelevant by construction, fields resolve to indices), extra columns added, one respondent answering twice, a literal duplicate row, an empty body, a header row with no data rows, HTML instead of CSV, and an email matching no student. THE DUPLICATE DECISION IS STATED AND TESTED: LAST WINS — a student who resubmits meant the second one, and treating the first as final would mark work they had already replaced; the losers are reported as `superseded_by_later_response` with the winning row's timestamp so "I resubmitted" can be checked rather than argued about. A same-second duplicate is a DIFFERENT fact (`duplicate_row_ref_in_batch`) because the row ref derives from (email, timestamp) and last-wins cannot tell them apart.
[2026-07-31 15:10] fix(submissions): a header row with no responses is HEALTHY, not an abort — why: it is the steady state of a Form nobody has answered yet. Aborting would put the hourly cron into permanent alarm over a sheet that is working perfectly, and an operator who learns to ignore the report page cannot be told anything by it later. `empty_csv` is now reserved for a body with no bytes at all, which IS a broken or unpublished sheet, and its advice says so. Tested at both: header-only reports rowsSeen 1 / blank_row 1 (the trailing newline's row is COUNTED rather than silently discounted, so "rows seen" matches what a human counts in the sheet) and aborted null; a header with no trailing newline reports rowsSeen 0 and no skips.
