[2026-07-31 15:20] feat(submissions): recording an ingest run is FAIL-SOFT and never propagates — why: the submission rows are already committed by the time it runs. Ingestion writes one statement per row precisely so that a bad row costs one row (ingest.ts header), and letting an audit line — a description of work that is already done and already visible to the student — turn a successful run into a 500 would invert that. It also means a checkout that has not created the table still ingests normally. The cost is that a missing report is ambiguous between "never ran" and "ran but could not be recorded"; the page STATES that ambiguity rather than resolving it silently, and the console line is the tie-breaker. All three of ingestAssignment's return paths go through one `finish()` wrapper so an early return cannot forget to record — the abort paths especially, since a no-op run is exactly the outcome nobody currently finds out about.
[2026-07-31 15:20] test(submissions): 18 unit cases for the verdict rule and the stored summary (ingest-status-view.test.ts), plus a live-database run of the whole path — why: the verdict ("healthy" / "stale" / "aborted: <reason>" / "never run" / "no sheet") is the only judgement this feature makes, and a judgement that can only be exercised by rendering a page in a browser is a judgement nobody tests. `now` is a parameter for the same reason. Precedence is asserted, not incidental: no sheet outranks everything (no run can EVER succeed), never-run outranks stale (there is no clock to measure), an abort outranks staleness (the abort is the cause, the staleness a symptom of the operator having stopped looking). Stale is >3 h against an hourly cron — two missed sweeps — rather than one, because alarming on a single missed serverless invocation trains the operator to ignore the page, which is the failure being fixed. selectSkippedSample keeps ONE OF EACH REASON before filling from the front: a plain slice() would spend the whole 20-row budget on trailing `blank_row`s and bury the single `supersedes_graded_submission` a human must act on. VERIFIED AGAINST THE LIVE DATABASE with a throwaway script driving the real ingestAssignment with an injected fetch: unknown_student, html_not_csv on both an honest text/html and a lying text/plain, empty_csv, header-only-healthy, a reordered+reworded header with a duplicate respondent (superseded_by_later_response 1, duplicate_row_ref_in_batch 1, unknown_student 1), and no_email_column — 0 submission rows written by any of them (asserted, not assumed), then recordIngestRun + getIngestStatus round-tripped and a second write confirmed to UPSERT rather than accumulate (1 row after 2 writes).
[2026-07-31 15:20] feat(submissions): scripts/migrate-ingest-runs.ts, an idempotent single-statement migration — why: `drizzle-kit push` diffs the WHOLE schema and would apply any other stream's pending change as a side effect of adding one additive table, and `strict: true` makes it want an interactive confirmation a non-interactive agent cannot give. This sends exactly the one CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS plus its index, then VERIFIES the result with an independent catalogue query rather than trusting the tool's success message — the habit scripts/verify-addon-schema.ts established in Wave 0. Not required for correctness (the recorder is fail-soft) and not the canonical path for a fresh checkout (that is db:push, or 0004 in CI); it exists so an already-seeded shared database can be brought up to date without a full push. RUN: reported "submission_ingest_runs is present and correctly shaped."
[2026-07-31 15:20] test(e2e): 7 specs for the operator surface in tests/e2e/submissions/submissions.spec.ts group 5 — AUTHORED, NOT RUN (nine agents, one port 3000, one shared seeded database; the coordinator runs e2e serially) — why: groups 1-4 prove the pipeline reports correctly and none of them proves the report reaches anybody. Covers the authorization boundary first, because the skipped-row samples carry other respondents' email addresses and a student must not see them (anonymous 401/403, student 403 on the API, student redirected off the page); that EVERY assignment appears including ones never ingested, since a row absent from the list is indistinguishable from a healthy one; that a manual ingest is recorded and its rowsSeen/aborted match what the trigger returned; and that the stand-in notice is present so a green verdict cannot be misread as a working Google pipeline. The testid sits on the Card element itself rather than an inner div — Card renders `title` in a header that is a SIBLING of children, which is exactly how a sibling stream lost 12 course-card specs today.
[2026-07-31 15:30] fix(scoring): an UNGRADED submission no longer awards the full 40 points — src/lib/contracts/scoring.ts assignmentPoints — why: it started at POINTS.ASSIGNMENT_MAX and only ever DEDUCTED (the late percentage, then 10 points per star below 3), so `stars: null` meaning "not yet rated" deducted nothing and returned 40. That was not a harmless default. src/lib/progress/score.ts assignmentPointsForWeek feeds the student dashboard and the weekly aggregate straight from this function, so the mere act of INGESTING a row from the response sheet awarded the student the full 40% of the week's assignment marks before any human had opened their work. A student who submitted an empty repository saw 40/40, and the number went DOWN when it was marked — the one direction a grade must never move for a reason the student cannot see. VERDICT: a real bug, fixed. Evidence it was known rather than accidental, and why "documented" did not make it right: progress/score.ts called it out as a "TRADE-OFF" and explicitly declined to work around it locally, correctly identifying it as a coordinated seam change; stand-in.test.ts carried a test named "an UNGRADED submission already scores the full assignment maximum" whose comment recorded that the author's first version asserted 0 and failed. So it was load-bearing on nothing and surprising to everyone who met it. No reading of the syllabus (Weekly Assignments 40%) awards 40% of a week for the act of submitting.
[2026-07-31 15:30] refactor(scoring): `assignmentPointsCeiling` split out of assignmentPoints — why: the two callers that WANTED the old null behaviour were both asking a different question — "what is still available to play for", not "what has been earned" — and were expressing it by passing a null and relying on what that used to mean. They are the student's "Maximum still available" badge (submissions/history.ts provisionalMaxScore, via the new ceilingForSubmission in lateness.ts) and the instructor queue's projection. One function per idea, so a caller states its intent in the call rather than in a convention about nulls. `stars` stays `number | null` deliberately: making it required would push the "is this graded?" decision out to every call site, which is exactly how two call sites come to disagree about it. A test asserts the ceiling equals assignmentPoints at 3 stars, so the two cannot drift apart silently.
[2026-07-31 15:30] test(scoring): the four tests that asserted the old behaviour are INVERTED, not deleted, and each says so at the point of inversion — why: they were faithful tests of a real defect and the next reader should be able to see that from the file rather than from `git log`. scoring.test.ts's "awards full marks on time with no rating yet" now asserts 0; its three late-penalty cases moved to 3-star inputs because they were using `stars: null` to isolate the late penalty, which is now what the ceiling function is for; lateness.test.ts's "an unrated submission shows the late-penalty ceiling" is now "awards nothing for an unrated submission, however late", paired with a new case that proves ceilingForSubmission still reports the ceiling; stand-in.test.ts's "an UNGRADED submission already scores the full assignment maximum" becomes "...scores NOTHING until an instructor rates it", which matters most there because the stand-in MANUFACTURES a response and its existence was therefore enough to award a student 40% of a week. New: a describe block of 5 cases for the ceiling function.
[2026-07-31 15:30] test(scoring): the CONSUMERS were run, not assumed — why: this change can silently move every student's total. `npx vitest run src/lib/leaderboard src/lib/penalties src/lib/progress src/lib/instructor src/lib/attendance` -> 18 files, 324 tests, ALL PASSED, with no edits needed to any of them. Full repository suite: 114 files, 1985 tests passed. The leaderboard is untouched by construction and this is worth stating: it is event-driven (onScoringEvent fires on a GRADING event, leaderboard/rebuild.ts sums stored `submissions.score`), so an ungraded submission never reached it and its numbers do not move. Progress DOES move, and in the intended direction — a student's weekly total now RISES when their work is marked, where before it could only fall. Penalties are unaffected: penalties/rules.ts keys off lateness and quiz percentages, not the assignment award. The grading write path always passes a real star rating (grade-payload.ts deriveScore takes `stars: number`), so a saved grade is unchanged.
[2026-07-31 15:40] fix(submissions): the stand-in Form URL is ORIGIN-RELATIVE, which is the real cause of the e2e failure at submissions.spec.ts:478 — why: standInFormUrl built an absolute URL from standInBaseUrl() (SUBMISSIONS_STAND_IN_BASE_URL, then NEXTAUTH_URL, then a literal), and that value is WRITTEN TO THE DATABASE at seed time and then served to whatever origin the student is actually browsing. Confirmed against the live database before changing anything: all four assignments held "http://localhost:3000/assignments/<n>/submit?standin=1", while playwright.config.ts serves the app on "http://127.0.0.1:3000". Clicking the link therefore left the origin, the host-only next-auth session cookie was not sent, requireUser() redirected to /login, and `getByTestId("stand-in-form-page")` never appeared. NOT merely a test-harness artefact: the same defect bites any origin the app is reached on that is not the one the database was seeded with — a Vercel preview deployment, a custom domain added later, www versus apex. A same-app link needs no origin at all; a relative path is correct on every origin at once and cannot go stale. The SHEET url stays absolute, deliberately, because its consumer is a server-side fetch which has no document to resolve a relative path against — that asymmetry is now the thing the tests assert.
[2026-07-31 15:40] fix(submissions): isStandInUrl parses a relative path, and this is load-bearing rather than tidying — why: it used `new URL(value)`, which THROWS on "/assignments/1/submit?standin=1" and was caught into `return false`. Left alone, the relative Form URL from the change above would have been classified as a real Google form, SubmitLink would have rendered the confident "Open the submission form" copy with no warning, and the student would have been sent to a page that says in three places that it is not a submission form. Both shapes are recognised, since databases seeded before this change still hold the absolute one. A bare "/assignments/1/submit" is still NOT a stand-in — the standin=1 flag decides, not the shape — and a protocol-relative "//host/path" is rejected outright so that the answer does not depend on the coincidence that such a value cannot carry our flag.
[2026-07-31 15:40] fix(seed): backfillAssignmentLinks now renormalises a STAND-IN Form URL whose shape is stale, which is the one documented exception to the `== null` rule — why: `== null` cannot repair a value that is already non-null and wrong, which is the same trap CHANGELOG records for the lectures.topic_key backfill. The rule that guard exists to protect is "an instructor's edit is final", and a stand-in URL is by definition not an instructor's edit — this repository wrote it and it says so in the value itself via the flag isStandInUrl tests. Anything WITHOUT that flag is still left strictly alone, so a real docs.google.com URL, or any other value a human typed, remains untouchable exactly as before. Stand-in -> stand-in only, and only when the stored shape differs from what the current revision produces. APPLIED to the shared database by a throwaway script mirroring this exact logic rather than by re-running the whole seeder against a database nine agents are using: all 4 rows rewritten to the relative form, sheet URLs untouched, isStandInUrl true on all 4 afterwards.
[2026-07-31 15:50] fix(e2e): submissions.spec.ts:507 failed on a Playwright fixture rule, not on the feature — why: it opened the second user with `page.context().browser()!.newPage()`, and a context created from the `browser` object DOES NOT INHERIT `use.baseURL` — Playwright applies context options in the `context`/`page` fixtures only. So `studentPage.goto("/assignments")` was a relative URL with no base and threw before any assertion in that block ran. A SECOND bug sat in the same six lines: it clicked the login submit button and immediately called goto, with no wait between, so the navigation could cancel the form POST before Set-Cookie landed and the next page would redirect to /login — indistinguishable from the feature being broken. Both are replaced by one `openSecondUser(browser, baseURL, role)` helper that passes baseURL explicitly and reuses `loginAs` from ../fixtures, which has always waited for the URL to leave /login. DEMO_PASSWORD is no longer imported by the file, with a comment saying why: it was only ever needed by the two hand-rolled logins, so a future import of it is the smell that a third has appeared.
