[2026-07-31 18:20] feat(notifications): history row is written BEFORE the job, and the preference gate runs before both — why: if the history INSERT fails, nothing exists and nothing was promised (the caller's committed quiz/exam/penalty is untouched, one log line names the student). If the ENQUEUE fails, the row survives at status='pending' — visible on the student's own page and to `select * from notifications where status='pending' and created_at < now() - interval '1 hour'` — so the message is LATE, not lost, and re-enqueueing the same key repairs it. The reverse order produces a dead-lettered job pointing at a row that does not exist, which is the one failure shape nobody can act on. Neither write joins the caller's transaction, for the reason src/lib/queue/store.ts#enqueueJob already argues: atomicity would mean a notification failure rolls back the student's submission. An opted-out student costs no row, no job and no drain slot; only the narrow case where the switch flips BETWEEN enqueue and drain records `suppressed`, and the handler re-reads preferences for exactly that window (a drain can be 5 minutes behind the enqueue on the GitHub cron).
