[2026-07-31 18:25] feat(prerequisites): cycles are PREVENTED, not merely reported — three overlapping layers — why: requirement 1 was "A requiring B requiring A must be impossible or detected", and "a DAG whose validity is only checked in the UI is not checked". Layer 1, the degenerate 1-cycle: a database CHECK (course_prerequisites_no_self, course_id <> prerequisite_course_id) makes a course requiring itself UNREPRESENTABLE — no code path, no backfill, no hand-written INSERT. Layer 2, longer cycles: insertPrerequisite (src/lib/prerequisites/store.ts) takes pg_advisory_xact_lock(84711230), RE-READS every edge inside the same transaction and re-runs wouldCreateCycle before writing. The LOCK is what makes this prevention rather than a race: validateNewPrerequisite decides against a snapshot read a round trip earlier (~245 ms of window, docs/SUBJECT_SECTIONS.md appendix), so two admins adding A->B and B->A inside that window would BOTH pass a check made against edges that did not yet contain the other's row, and no single-row constraint can see a two-row cycle. The result would be a pair of courses neither of which can ever be entered, created by two people each told their edit was fine. Same reasoning as decideRequest's compare-and-set at src/lib/courses/store.ts:346 — a check-then-act needs the act scoped to the state the check was made against, and where no row carries that state, a lock is how you scope it. Chose an advisory lock over SERIALIZABLE because the conflicting pair writes two DIFFERENT rows (no predicate conflict Postgres tracks here) and raising the isolation level would force every caller into a retry loop; the cost is that prerequisite writes are globally serialised, which is free at admin-authoring speed. Layer 3, the tripwire: findCycle runs on /admin/prerequisites and names the cycle as a defect banner, because a non-null result means the data came from outside the application (a restored dump, a DBA's INSERT) and an unreachable course must not look like a mystery. Every traversal in graph.ts also carries a visited set and is ITERATIVE, not recursive: the failure mode of an unguarded walk is a hung request on a STUDENT'S page — an unexplained lockout with no message — and a 500-deep chain in a recursive walk is a RangeError inside a gate, where the surrounding catch decides the access outcome. VERIFIED: 30 unit tests in src/lib/prerequisites/graph.test.ts over self-edges, two-course cycles, cycles closed through a 5-link chain, diamonds (which must be ALLOWED — "two courses both need the fundamentals course" is the most ordinary shape there is), disconnected components, non-mutation of the caller's edge list, termination on an ALREADY cyclic graph, and a 500-node chain. UNVERIFIED: the advisory lock itself. Proving it needs two concurrent transactions against a real database, the tables are not migrated yet, and I am forbidden from generating the migration.
[2026-07-31 18:25] feat(prerequisites): the override is VISIBLE rather than silent, in four places at once — why: requirement 4 was that an admin can admit a student despite an unmet prerequisite AND that the exception is not silent. Folding it into course_access_requests.status was rejected: that column already means "an admin approved this request", and approval happens BEFORE a prerequisite may exist, so every pre-existing approval would silently BECOME an override the moment a rule landed and the console could not tell "met the requirement" from "was let through anyway". A boolean folded into someone else's status column is the definition of silent. So: a second table whose `reason` is NOT NULL (an unexplained override cannot be stored by any route, including a hand-rolled POST to the compiled action); `unmet_at_grant`, a text snapshot of what was actually waived, written once and NEVER consulted by the gate, so an auditor can still answer "what did this admin wave through" after the rules have changed; granted_by/granted_at naming the admin and the moment, with revoked rows KEPT and stamped rather than deleted behind a PARTIAL unique index (revoked_at IS NULL) that allows at most one LIVE override while preserving the history a plain unique index would force you to delete; and — the part an admin console alone cannot give — the STUDENT's own course page says they are admitted by an override and quotes the reason, because a record only the granter can read is silent to the person it is about, and a student who believes they earned a prerequisite they did not will be surprised by the next course that cites it. evaluatePrerequisites deliberately KEEPS the unmet list populated when overridden (satisfied is the decision, unmet is the truth, and the two may differ as long as both are shown), and grantPrerequisiteOverrideAction RECOMPUTES what is unmet on merit and refuses `nothing_unmet` — a no-op override row would read on the console as an exception that was grante