[2026-07-31 18:53] feat(activity): the audit write is FAIL-CLOSED for critical actions — the act rolls back if its record cannot be written — why: this is the decision the feature turns on, so it is a decision with a stated reason rather than wherever the try/catch landed. recordActivity takes a database client; a caller inside db.transaction() passes its tx and the row becomes one more statement in the same unit of work, so the act commits if and only if its audit row commits. The usual instinct ("logging must never break the app") answers the wrong question here: the premise of an audit trail is that the record and the act are the same event, so a system performing graded, irreversible, disputable acts while its trail is broken is not degraded — it is producing exactly the state an audit exists to rule out, outcomes nobody can account for. A missing row is indistinguishable from an act that never happened, so an appeal is decided on data with an unexplained gap; and the failure is silent precisely when it matters most, because a broad outage means MANY missing rows, not one. THE COST IS AFFORDABLE FOR ONE STRUCTURAL REASON, stated so a future reader can check whether it still holds: the INSERT runs on the SAME connection inside the SAME transaction as the act, so if it cannot execute then the act's own statements could not have executed either — it is not a new dependency, it is one more statement in a unit of work that already had to succeed or fail as a whole. Were the sink elsewhere (a log service, a second database, a queue) failing closed WOULD couple availability to an unrelated system and the answer would have to change. Routine high-volume actions (code_execute above all, which a live editor fires many times a minute) use recordActivityDetached, which swallows, counts and never throws — and REFUSES a critical action at runtime, so the cheap path is unreachable for the events that need the expensive one. NOT ROUTED THROUGH src/lib/queue: enqueueJob is itself one INSERT on the same pool, so queueing avoids no round trip (~245 ms warm, src/db/index.ts:63) while adding failure modes — a job that exhausts its attempts becomes status='dead', and an audit entry that can be dead-lettered is not an audit entry; the row would also land seconds-to-minutes late, out of order, from a different clock. NOT BUFFERED IN MEMORY: FREE_STACK commits this to Vercel, where there is no long-lived process, so a timer-flushed buffer loses whatever it held when the invocation is recycled.

[2026-07-31 18:54] feat(activity): one redaction choke point, and what this table will never hold — why: "just log everything leaks" is not hypothetical in this repository. A sibling agent found getAtRiskStudents selecting users.email and src/components/analytics/AnalyticsPanels.tsx:181 RENDERING it on a staff page — nobody decided to publish student addresses, a query selected the whole row and a component rendered what it was handed; separately, in `next dev` React serialises the awaited session object including email into the RSC payload embedded in the HTML (tests/e2e/fixtures.ts:39-47). Both are the same failure, and an audit table is the worst place for it: written from everywhere, kept 90 days, read in bulk through a UI with an export button on it. So nothing reaches details/ip_prefix/client_family except through src/lib/activity/redact.ts, which is a pure function with 81 assertions against it. NOT RECORDED, EVER: passwords and hashes; session tokens, JWTs, cookies, authorization headers, CSRF and reset tokens, API keys; request bodies and query strings IN ANY FORM — sanitiseDetails takes a FLAT record and drops every nested value, so spreading a body into it yields nothing usable rather than a leak, which is structural rather than a rule to remember; email addresses and personal names (the actor is a foreign key, the address is joined at read time for a caller who has already passed requireRole("admin"), so no copy here can outlive account deletion); quiz and exam answers, submitted work, instructor feedback, anything a human wrote; full IP addresses (a /24 or /48 network prefix only — the fraud question is "did these two accounts act from the same network", which a /24 answers, whereas a full address answers "which subscriber" and has no extra audit value); full User-Agent strings (a coarse family with NO version numbers, and an unrecognised header is stored as "Unrecognised client", keeping no fragment of an attacker-controlled string); exception messages and stack traces. Erasure is reconciled with retention by NOT copying identity: deleting a user pseudonymises their trail rather than leaving a snapshot of their address behind for the whole window — cost stated, after a deletion you can still see that someone with role student did these things in this order, but not who.

[2026-07-31 18:55] fix(activity): a partial unique index means ON CONFLICT must repeat its predicate, or NOTHING can be logged — why: found by executing the real DDL and the real statement against Postgres in a throwaway schema, not by typechecking, because both versions typecheck identically. "on conflict (dedupe_key) do nothing" against "create unique index ... where dedupe_key is not null" fails with 42P10, "there is no unique or exclusion constraint matching the ON CONFLICT specification" — so the defect was not "duplicates slip through", it was EVERY insert failing: an audit trail that records nothing while looking correct in review. record.ts now passes the index predicate as the `where` option and drizzle's rendered SQL was printed to confirm the clause lands in the target position rather than after DO NOTHING. The index stays PARTIAL rather than plain because Postgres already allows unlimited NULLs in a plain unique index, so a plain one would behave identically while carrying an entry for every row of the largest table in the database to constrain the handful that have a key — measured 16 kB partial, against the 8792 kB the primary key costs over the same 400_000 rows.

[2026-07-31 18:56] perf(activity): index strategy MEASURED on 400_000 rows, and the second column changed because of what it showed — why: this table is expected to be the largest in the database, so an index serving no query is write amplification on every logged act, and the roadmap's list (userIdIdx, createdAtIdx, entityIdx) was checked rather than adopted. Ran the real DDL in a throwaway schema on this project's Neon instance with 400_000 rows over 2_000 actors and `explain (analyze, buffers)` per query shape. (1) The composites are now (actor_id, id DESC) and (action, id DESC), NOT (col, occurred_at DESC): the list query orders by `id` because a bigserial is unique and monotonic so the keyset cursor cannot be ambiguous, and with occurred_at as the second column Postgres used the index for the FILTER and still had to SORT — the second column has to be the one the query actually orders by. Actor+keyset is now an Index Scan at 0.102 ms / 54 buffers; at 60_000 rows over 40 actors the planner preferred a backward primary-key scan instead, which is the planner being right at 1-in-40 selectivity rather than the index being wrong, since pk-scan cost grows with the number of accounts. (2) BRIN on occurred_at instead of the roadmap's btree: 24 kB against 8792 kB for the equivalent btree on the same table, and the BRIN did not grow between the 60k and 400k runs while the btree grew linearly — near-optimal precisely because this table is append-only, never updated and inserted in time order, so physical order matches logical order. (3) HONESTLY REPORTED: the planner did NOT choose (action, id DESC) for action-plus-24h-window; it filtered on the time window and sorted the ~300 survivors (0.458 ms), so that index is kept for the wide-window shape and its comment says to drop it if neither shape ever uses it. No GIN on details (it would be the most expensive index in the database and nothing queries inside it); status deliberately unindexed (two values is never selective enough to beat filtering rows another index already narrowed). Reads are bounded: page size clamped to 200, export to 20_000 rows, keyset paging rather than OFFSET so a row written mid-page cannot shift the window and hide an entry, and the size tile uses pg_class.reltuples rather than count(*) — an exact count on every admin page load is the likeliest way this feature becomes the performance problem it exists to watch.

[2026-07-31 18:57] feat(activity): retention defaults to the spec's 90 days with a 30-day FLOOR, and the scheduled prune is a DRY RUN — why: INTEGRATION_SUMMARY.md:115 states "keep 90 days hot, auto-archive older", so 90 is the default rather than a number invented here. Three places the spec and this stack disagree, resolved in the open. (a) There is no cold storage: @vercel/blob is a proposed dependency and is not installed, and the free stack has no object store — so a prune DELETES, the CSV export is the archive path, and pruneActivity refuses to run without confirm=exported so a schedule cannot delete an unarchived trail by accident. (b) 90 days is SHORTER than this cohort's audit horizon: a course runs for weeks and appeals arrive after it ends, so a dispute about week 1 can fall outside the window; ACTIVITY_RETENTION_DAYS overrides it and 400 days (a year plus 35 of grace) is documented as the recommended production value, so following the spec is a choice rather than an accident. (c) A 30-day floor is enforced in code because ACTIVITY_RETENTION_DAYS=1 would erase almost the whole trail on the next run with no undo, and a malformed value falls back to the DEFAULT rather than to a smaller window — failing towards keeping data is the only safe direction. Parsing is decimal-digits-only, because Number("1e3") is 1000 and Number("0x5A") is 90: both are integers, so an isInteger check alone accepts a window nobody typed. Deletion runs in 5_000-row batches under a self-imposed 20_000 ms budget, because one DELETE over millions of rows holds a transaction open for minutes and is killed at the platform's wall-clock limit, rolling the work back and repeating it forever. The prune writes an activity_pruned row BEFORE deleting — the same pre-write ordering as the mail ledger (schema.queue.ts:35-47) and for the same reason: if the pre-write fails nothing has happened yet, so refusing is completely safe. The vercel.json entry is the DRY RUN, deliberately: a Vercel cron cannot supply the confirmation flag, so the schedule reports how many rows are past the window and deletes nothing until an operator exports and asks. Monthly range partitioning is documented as the next step rather than built, and is cheap to adopt later precisely because of the append-only choices above.

[2026-07-31 18:58] feat(activity): /admin/activity plus a read-only API, and NEITHER src/lib/guard.ts NOR src/middleware.ts was instrumented — why: the brief named both as the natural place to observe actor identity; both were considered and rejected on evidence, written up in docs/ACTIVITY_LOGS.md section 7. middleware.ts runs on the EDGE runtime and cannot reach Postgres — that is the entire reason it verifies the JWT with next-auth/jwt rather than importing src/lib/auth.ts (its own header, lines 10-18) — so no sink is available to it. guard.ts is called several times per request (the (staff) layout and then the page each call requireRole, and RSC re-renders call it again), so per-call logging yields duplicate rows and a PAGE-VIEW log: a different artefact, orders of magnitude larger, answering a different question, with a database write on the render path of every page. Instrumenting only the denial branch looks attractive and is worse: middleware denies at the edge BEFORE guard.ts is reached for every prefix in its PROTECTED table, so requireRole's forbidden branch fires only for pages under an UNLISTED prefix — it would capture a small, unpredictable subset while looking like "all refusals", which is worse than a known absence. What the admin surface does instead: chip filters are LINKS, so a filtered view is a shareable URL (an investigator can paste "every failed login for actor 7 between these dates" into a ticket) and the export takes exactly that query string, so the file matches the screen it was taken from. A MISTYPED FILTER IS REJECTED, never silently widened — ?action=logn renders an error rather than the whole table, because an admin who searched for an event and was shown everything concludes the event never happened, and in an investigation that false negative is worse than an error message (the same rule as api/admin/jobs/route.ts:44-49). The export records an activity_export row BEFORE emitting bytes and returns 503 if that row cannot be written: no record, no export. There is deliberately NO route that writes a log entry, and an e2e spec asserts POST /api/admin/activity does not exist, so a future convenience handler fails the suite. CSV cells are neutralised against formula injection unconditionally (a leading =+-@ is evaluated by Excel and Sheets, and =WEBSERVICE/=HYPERLINK exfiltration has a long history) rather than only on the columns currently believed to be untrusted. Three ROUTE_AUTH entries were added to the frozen map on the async-queues precedent: enforcement is unchanged, since all three handlers already took their level from apiGuard/requireCron, but an unlisted route has no ROUTE_AUTH entry and so cannot be caught by that map's own exhaustiveness check — a particularly bad omission for the two routes that read and delete the trail.

[2026-07-31 18:59] docs(activity): the feature ships SAYING what it does not yet cover, against the roadmap's "100% of actions" metric — why: the write paths that would emit login, quiz_submit, submission_graded and the rest live in src/lib/auth.ts, the quiz submit handler, the grading handler and the admin server actions — files this stream does not own, in a wave where eight agents are editing concurrently. Live call sites in this commit are activity_export, activity_export_denied and activity_pruned: the audit trail auditing itself. Everything else is declared vocabulary plus a hook point in src/lib/activity/hook-points.ts, which is DATA keyed on the frozen route map and asserted against ROUTES, so the plan cannot describe routes that no longer exist — the alternative, a prose TODO reading "hook into all API endpoints that modify data", cannot fail when a route is renamed and therefore drifts in the direction of looking done. Each entry names the route, the action, the entity type, whether the row must be written inside the act's transaction, and which details keys to pass. hook-points.test.ts additionally asserts that no planned details key is one the redactor would silently drop, which CAUGHT A REAL DEFECT: `rowsSkipped` was being thrown away because the denylist matched "ip" as a SUBSTRING (it also rejected recipientCount and description), so the list is now split into long unambiguous substrings plus whole-token matching on the short ambiguous names — over-blocking is not a safe failure here, it makes the trail quietly less useful while looking like it works. unwiredActions() derives the gap from the code and /admin/activity prints it on screen: a gap an operator knows about is one they can work around, whereas a gap they believe is coverage is a wrong conclusion in an investigation. Also declared out of scope rather than half-built: tamper-evidence against a compromised database (hash chaining, an append-only replica, shipping rows off-box), because anything holding the app's credentials can INSERT or DELETE directly and no application-level design changes that.

[2026-07-31 18:47] feat(forums): two tables in a NEW sibling module src/db/schema.forums.ts (`forum_topics`, `forum_posts`) plus one line in drizzle.config.ts — why: schema.ts is the frozen Wave 0 seam and eight agents hold it open, so the established sibling-module pattern (schema.access.ts:6, schema.queue.ts:2, and drizzle.config.ts:9-14's own comment) is used instead of the roadmap's "add to src/db/schema.ts". FOUR DELIBERATE DEPARTURES from the roadmap's snippet (IMPLEMENTATION_ROADMAP.md:389-415), each argued at the column: (1) `serial` INTEGER keys, not `uuid` — the snippet's `weekId: uuid('week_id').references(() => weeks.id)` does not compile, because weeks.id is `serial` (schema.ts:137) and a uuid FK to a serial PK is a type error; every table in the repo keys on serial. (2) NO `postCount` and NO `lastPostAt` columns — both DERIVED in SQL instead. This is the N+1 answer, and dropping the counters is what makes it safe rather than what makes it slow: five writers would have to maintain post_count and TWO OF THEM ARE POSTGRES CASCADES (user delete, topic delete), which will not run application code, so the counter is wrong the first time an account is deleted and wrong SILENTLY — a topic reading "12 replies" that shows 9. The aggregate is free: docs/SUBJECT_SECTIONS.md measures a 4-row aggregate at the same ~245 ms as `SELECT 1`, so a LEFT JOIN + GROUP BY inside a statement already being issued adds no round trip. Cost stated: ORDER BY max(created_at) cannot use an index, so it is a sort over one week's topics. (3) NO `upvotes` column — the roadmap describes no voting UI, no vote table and no per-user uniqueness, so an integer with no writer would read as a feature while being unable to stop one student voting a thousand times. (4) tombstone columns ADDED (`removed_at`, `removed_by`, `removal_reason`), which the roadmap specifies nowhere — INTEGRATION_SUMMARY.md:324 names moderation only as a risk mitigation.

[2026-07-31 18:47] feat(forums): removal is a TOMBSTONE, never a DELETE, and the removed body is withheld by SQL rather than by the caller — why: three independent reasons, and "just delete the row" fails all three. (a) A hard delete DESTROYS THE THREAD'S MEANING: posts are flat and chronological and replies answer each other by position ("the second point above is wrong"), so deleting post 3 of 7 silently rewrites what posts 4-7 appear to be replying to. (b) The row IS the audit record — `removed_by`/`removed_at` are the only evidence a moderator ever acted, and a student who believes a removal was unfair has nothing to appeal against once it is gone. This is the argument this repo already makes twice in the same words (schema.access.ts:99 "a rejected row is KEPT, never deleted... deleting the row would erase the only evidence that an admin ever looked at it", and the topic_videos review flow). (c) `is_solution` would DANGLE — a thread claiming to be solved by nothing. WHAT MAKES THE TOMBSTONE HONEST rather than a way to keep serving removed content: `listPosts` selects `CASE WHEN removed_at IS NULL THEN content ELSE NULL END`, so the body never leaves Postgres. That is strictly stronger than filtering in the caller, because a caller that forgets to filter is a bug that COMPILES; here no code path — not a component mistake, not a future JSON endpoint — can surface the text. VERIFIED against the real database: a removed post came back with content NULL, still listed (3 rows), attributed `removedByModerator: true`, reason surfaced. A student's own retraction writes the SAME tombstone with removed_by = author_id, deliberately: the alternative lets an author post abuse and then erase the proof.

[2026-07-31 18:47] feat(forums): XSS defence is FIVE LAYERS and none of them filters the input — why: this is the first surface in the app where one student's text renders in another student's browser (every other markdown body is staff-authored), so the threat model changes even though the renderer does not. (1) src/components/course/MarkdownContent.tsx is REUSED VERBATIM — not reimplemented, not re-configured — because `rehype-raw` is not enabled, so embedded HTML is never parsed into an element and there is no element for an `onerror` to attach to. Reuse is itself part of the defence: a second renderer would be a second place for rehype-raw to be added by someone who did not read the comment. (2) react-markdown's `defaultUrlTransform` (node_modules/react-markdown/lib/index.js:416, v9.1.0) empties any href/src whose scheme is not in its allowlist, so `[x](javascript:alert(1))` renders href="". (3) NO dangerouslySetInnerHTML anywhere in the stream — titles, author names and removal reasons are React text children — asserted MECHANICALLY by a scan over src/lib/forums, src/components/forums and src/app/(app)/forums. (4) NOTHING is sanitised on the way in: escaping at write time corrupts the source ("why does `a < b` fail?" stored as "a &lt; b", re-escaped to "a &amp;lt; b" on the author's next edit) and is the wrong layer for the same text later emailed or exported. (5) a removed body is not in the process at all (see the tombstone entry). PAYLOADS TESTED, 11 raw-HTML + 6 URL: `<img src=x onerror=>` (plus an uppercase self-closing variant), `<script>`, `<script src=>`, `<svg onload=>`, `<div onmouseover=>`, `<iframe>`, `<a href=javascript:>`, `<body onload=>`, `<input onfocus= autofocus>`, an HTML-comment breakout, and `javascript:` / `JaVaScRiPt:` / `data:text/html;base64` / `vbscript:` on both links and images.

[2026-07-31 18:47] test(forums): every XSS assertion is against the DOM, never against HTML text, because on this renderer a string assertion is either WRONG or VACUOUS — why: react-markdown without rehype-raw renders embedded HTML as LITERAL TEXT, so `<img src=x onerror="alert(1)">` becomes `&lt;img src=x onerror="alert(1)"&gt;` in the markup. Read what that does to the two obvious string tests. `expect(innerHTML).not.toContain("onerror")` FAILS on output that is completely safe — the substring is present precisely BECAUSE the payload is being displayed as text — and a developer seeing that red would weaken the check. `expect(innerHTML).toContain("&lt;img")` PASSES VACUOUSLY: it is true of escapeHtml() applied to anything, including output that also contains a live injected element elsewhere in the tree. A sibling stream in this wave hit the first form. So every assertion asks a STRUCTURAL question (querySelector, getAttribute, tagName, href). And because a suite of toBeNull() passes just as well against a renderer that emits NOTHING, the file carries two kinds of control: POSITIVE ones (`**bold**` still yields <strong>, an https link still yields a live href with rel=noopener, a fenced html block still renders as code) and a DETECTION control that renders the same payload through dangerouslySetInnerHTML and asserts the negative helpers THROW against it — proving the assertions can see an injection, the one thing a passing negative can never demonstrate about itself. The mechanical no-sink scan also needed a comment stripper: its first run FAILED on ForumPostViewer.tsx because that file's header DISCUSSES dangerouslySetInnerHTML and rehype-raw in order to explain why neither is used, and a substring scan cannot tell an explanation from a call. Recorded rather than worked around silently — the alternatives were deleting the explanation to appease a grep, or a per-file exemption that would also excuse a real call.

[2026-07-31 18:47] perf(forums): thread lists aggregate in SQL — 3 statements at sequential depth 1 for ANY number of threads, against 1+N for the obvious shape — why: commit 25fe2d2 measured a warm Neon round trip at ~245 ms and concluded that "query complexity is irrelevant... the metric that matters is a page's SEQUENTIAL DEPTH". A 20-thread list doing `SELECT count(*) FROM forum_posts WHERE topic_id = $1` per row is 21 round trips = ~5.1 SECONDS, and it would pass review — one small query in a .map(). MEASURED BUDGET: /forums = 3 statements depth 1 (getWeekList's 2, internally concurrent, plus ONE aggregate carrying the topic count for every week); /forums/:weekId = 3 depth 1 for 20 threads AND for 200, because reply count, last-activity and solved-flag are aggregates in one GROUP BY; /forums/:weekId/:topicId = 4 depth 1 (author names JOINed, not looked up per post — the other classic N+1 here); a mutation = 2 depth 2, which cannot be less because it must read the row's authorization facts before it may write. Content reads are issued CONCURRENTLY with `gateWeek` and this does not weaken the gate: the rows are discarded UNREAD when the gate refuses, the same argument gateLecture makes at data.ts:395. Two arithmetic traps that would send a reader back to the N+1 are pinned in comments and were VERIFIED against real Postgres: `count(DISTINCT t.id)` (the LEFT JOIN to posts fans each topic out once per reply, so a plain count reports a 9-reply topic as 9 topics — verified 2, not 4) and `AND p.removed_at IS NULL` in the JOIN predicate rather than the WHERE clause (moving it to WHERE silently turns the LEFT JOIN into an inner one and hides every unanswered thread — verified a zero-reply topic still listed).

[2026-07-31 18:47] feat(forums): authorization reuses `gateWeek` for reads and adds a pure policy module for writes; a moderator may REMOVE a post but may NOT EDIT one — why: threads are anchored to a WEEK precisely so that "may this student read this thread?" is a question the app already answers. gateWeek (data.ts:338) resolves course access (a week of another course is simply absent from the list it searches, so it returns not_found — indistinguishable from a nonexistent id, which is what stops a probe enumerating another course's weeks), the section-release switch, and quiz progression, as ONE answer. Re-deriving forum visibility would have created a second rule for "can this student see week 3", and the copy that drifts is always the one guarding the deeper URL (data.ts:350). Withdrawing the CSS3 subject closes its discussion automatically, because nothing in the stream knows what a subject is. THE WRITE RULES are pure functions in src/lib/forums/policy.ts with no DB, session or next/* import, so the negatives are asserted as properties rather than inferred from a button not rendering — the same split src/lib/courses/policy.ts:6-12 makes. No role string is compared to a literal; every role question goes through roleSatisfies and the frozen ROLES_SATISFYING table, which is why an admin moderates without anyone remembering to add them. MODERATION IS SET AT "instructor" (= instructor + admin), the OPPOSITE call to courses' admin-only approval, because taking down an abusive reply is a TEACHING act on content inside a week the instructor already grades, whereas approving course access is an ENROLMENT act that changes the leaderboard population; moderation that must be escalated is moderation that happens tomorrow. A MODERATOR MAY NOT EDIT: removal is attributable (removed_by is recorded and the reader is told) but an edit is not — a rewritten post is still attributed to the student, with nothing distinguishing their words from the instructor's, which is putting words in a student's mouth under their name and is not recoverable by them. A locked thread refuses replies from STAFF TOO: "closed for you, open for me" is what makes a lock read as censorship. Refusal ORDER is the security order — `not_author` is checked before every state check, so a student probing a guessed post id cannot learn whether it is removed or its thread locked, and the two "someone else's row" messages say the same thing so a guessed id is not a membership oracle.

[2026-07-31 18:47] feat(forums): server actions, not the roadmap's API routes, and three independent locks on the edit path — why: ROUTES in src/lib/contracts/api.ts is frozen and lists no forum endpoint, so adding `POST /api/forum/topics` (IMPLEMENTATION_ROADMAP.md:422) would create a path with no ROUTE_AUTH entry — the unguarded-by-omission bug that map exists to prevent, and what its own header (api.ts:61) calls a defeat of the compile-time check. Editing a frozen cross-stream contract while seven agents hold the same file open is the collision this wave is structured to avoid. Same call the courses stream made (courses/actions.ts:8) and the video-ingestion stream before it, so it is the house pattern. Consequence for middleware: ONE row (`/forums`, "student") and no /api/forums prefix, because actions POST to the page's own path. EVERY action opens with a guard that RE-RUNS THE WEEK GATE, because a write arrives without a matching read in three real ways: the subject was withheld by a config deploy while the tab stayed open, a quiz retake changed progression, or the action was simply called directly — it is an HTTP POST endpoint and nothing requires it to have been reached from a page, which alone makes the re-check mandatory. THE EDIT PATH HAS THREE LOCKS because any one could be removed by a future edit: canEditPost refuses a non-author; `updatePostContent` carries `author_id = <session user>` in its WHERE clause so a bypassed policy call matches zero rows; and the session id comes from the cookie, never from the form. VERIFIED against real Postgres: the non-author UPDATE matched 0 rows and the author's matched 1. `insertPost` likewise carries `WHERE EXISTS (... is_locked = false)` so a moderator's lock landing inside the ~245 ms read-then-write window still takes effect — verified: the INSERT into a locked thread matched 0 rows.

[2026-07-31 18:47] feat(forums): routes are /forums, /forums/:weekId and /forums/:weekId/:topicId, NOT the roadmap's (app)/weeks/[weekId]/forum — why: two reasons, both about what exists. (a) A NAV HREF CANNOT CONTAIN A DYNAMIC SEGMENT — there is no week number that is right for every student — and tests/unit/cross-stream-contracts.test.ts walks src/app and fails any nav href with no page.tsx behind it, a guard that exists because the /course-vs-/weeks typo 404'd silently for every student. A forum reachable only at /weeks/3/forum has no nav row it can legally have, so it would be reachable only by typing a URL, against this feature's own success metric of 50+ posts/week. (b) src/app/(app)/weeks/** is the course-content stream's segment and the parent owns routing under it. The information architecture the spec asks for is unchanged — forums are week-scoped, per the schema's week_id — only the segment moved. The index page sits in an `(index)` ROUTE GROUP with its loading.tsx, copying src/app/(app)/weeks/(index)/: a loading.tsx is a Suspense boundary covering everything nested beneath it, and a boundary at the `forums` segment would swallow the 404s of /forums/:weekId and /forums/:weekId/:topicId — which could NOT have been guarded from above, because a layout at /forums is never handed a [weekId]. A route group is not part of the URL, so /forums still resolves while [weekId] is a SIBLING the boundary cannot reach. The loading.tsx was written by this stream despite routing not being ours, for the reason src/app/(app)/badges/loading.tsx records in this same wave: boundary-scope.test.ts requires every sidebar destination to have one, so the choice was between that file and leaving the feature undiscoverable. It carries no routing decision — two lines delegating to PageSkeleton, with the shape decided by the new "/forums" rule in loading-shape.ts. The two dynamic routes deliberately get NO boundary, since nothing requires one and adding one would mean also adding the layout.tsx guard that keeps their notFound() honest, and layout.tsx belongs to the routing owner.

[2026-07-31 18:47] docs(forums): reply notifications are NOT implemented, and the extension path is written down instead — why: the brief made them conditional ("if you notify on replies, go through the existing queue and the mail_dispatches ledger"), and shipping them requires editing src/lib/queue/types.ts (JOB_KINDS) and src/lib/queue/registry.ts, both owned by the async-queues stream, in a wave where concurrent edits to shared files are the failure being avoided. No second mail path and no second scheduler was added — which is the constraint that actually mattered. THE PATH, for whoever picks it up: one member added to JOB_KINDS, one entry in the Record<JobKind, JobHandler> in registry.ts (the type makes a missing handler a COMPILE error, not a job that dead-letters in production), one key minter in queue/keys.ts, and one handler. The key must be scoped to the POST id, not the topic — keying on the topic means only the first reply ever notifies, silently, which is the mistake gradedNotificationKey's header (keys.ts) argues through for grading. Recipients are the topic author plus everyone who has posted in the thread, minus the new post's own author. Note also that `notification_preferences.forumReply` already exists in the notifications stream's table (IMPLEMENTATION_ROADMAP.md:82), so the opt-out column is waiting for a producer.

[2026-07-31 19:05] feat(peer-review): four tables in a sibling schema module, with no-self-review as a Postgres CHECK — why: roadmap feature 6 (roadmap:427) sketches `peer_reviews` and `grading_rubrics` and is silent on the three questions the feature actually turns on, so `peer_review_rounds` (K, allocated_at, released_at) and `peer_review_allocations` (who reviews whom) are added and argued in the module header. Sibling module plus ONE line in drizzle.config.ts, per that file's own rule and the five precedents beside it; the coordinator generates the wave's single migration. TWO DELIBERATE DEVIATIONS FROM THE SKETCH, both stated in the file. (1) serial integer PKs, not uuid (roadmap:433): every table in schema.ts is serial and a uuid island would be the only table an operator cannot name by a number they can read aloud — brief says prefer what exists. (2) `peer_reviews` HAS NO reviewer_id COLUMN, though roadmap:439 puts one there. That is the shape in which anonymity leaks: the reviewee-facing read model is a query over peer_reviews, `select()` with no projection returns every column, and one careless edit hands the reviewer's id to the person they reviewed. The reviewer is reachable only by joining peer_review_allocations, which a leak would have to do deliberately. `peer_review_allocations` carries reviewee_id DENORMALIZED beside reviewer_id for one reason: it lets CHECK (reviewer_id <> reviewee_id) exist, because a row-level CHECK cannot see submissions.student_id one join away. The redundancy IS the feature — every other guarantee in this stream is application code a future edit can bypass; that one cannot be bypassed by any INSERT from any client.

[2026-07-31 19:05] feat(peer-review): allocation is a seeded ring with fixed offsets, pure and property-tested over cohort sizes 0..40 — why: the brief asked for deterministic and fair, and both are properties of a PERMUTATION, so planAllocations takes (submissions, K, seed) and touches no database — which is what lets 172 tests assert every property for EVERY n from 0 to 40 rather than for one hand-picked cohort, in 67 ms. THE RULE: submitters sorted by student id, Fisher-Yates-shuffled by a mulberry32 seeded from assignments.id, then reviewer at ring position i reviews positions (i+1)..(i+K) mod n. Four properties become structural rather than checked-afterwards: no self-review (offsets are 1..K with K <= n-1, so (i+d) mod n is never i — no rejection loop that could fail to terminate), no duplicate pair, exactly K reviews RECEIVED per submission, and exactly K WRITTEN per reviewer — "you are reviewed by as many people as you review", which a random matching does not give at these cohort sizes. Seeded from the assignment and not a clock because re-running allocation must be a RECONCILE: a timestamp seed re-pairs everyone who has already started reading somebody's work. mulberry32 is twelve hand-written lines rather than a dependency for the reason keys.ts refuses hashing — a library that changes its stream between minor versions silently re-pairs every historical round. DEGENERATE CASES, both asked for and both reported rather than silent: a student with NO submission is in the ring neither as reviewer nor as reviewee (stated cost: they get no feedback at all; preferred to asking students who did the work to review for someone who did not, and a late submission brings them in on the next reconcile), and a cohort too small DEGRADES K to n-1 with `degraded: true` so the surface can say "2 requested, 1 possible with 2 submissions" instead of quietly allocating fewer; at n <= 1 nothing is allocated and the reason is `cohort_too_small`, because the only available reviewer is the author.

[2026-07-31 19:05] feat(peer-review): reviews are anonymous TO THE REVIEWEE and the data model enforces it — why: roadmap:758 names anonymity as the mitigation for bias in peer grading, and a comment saying so enforces nothing. THREE LAYERS, in the order they bite: (1) peer_reviews has no reviewer_id to select; (2) the reviewee-facing type `RevealedReview` has no field an identity could travel in, so leaking one means widening a type — a visible act in a diff; (3) reviews.anonymity.test.ts hands the read model rows that DO carry the reviewer's id, name and email — exactly what a careless `select()` produces — then serialises the RETURN VALUE and fails if any of the three appears at any depth, plus a loop over REVIEWER_IDENTITY_FIELDS asserting none of them is a KEY. Asserted on the response body, not on prose. NOT DOUBLE-BLIND, AND THE CLAIM IS NOT MADE: the reviewer opens submissions.github_url, and a GitHub URL contains its owner's account name. So this stream does not SEND the author's name, email or id to the reviewer (asserted), and the reviewer can often work out who they are anyway (stated). The honest description is single-blind-enforced plus best-effort reviewer-side blinding that the artefact defeats; making it real would mean proxying student repositories. Reviews are also labelled POSITIONALLY to the reviewee ("Anonymous review 1 of 2") and never by peer_reviews.id, because a real id is a global sequence — two students comparing the ids they received could narrow down who reviewed whom, and a student given ids 41 and 63 learns how many reviews the cohort wrote in between.

[2026-07-31 19:05] feat(peer-review): the reveal point is an instructor switch, not a deadline — why: a calendar reveal fires unattended, which means the first person to read a one-word review is the student it is about. `peer_review_rounds.released_at` NULL means no student may read any review of their own work, whatever the date; setting it is an instructor act, and that human gate IS the gaming defence. Follows the release-switch pattern already on this branch (e4c0329, subject sections). The rule lives in ONE pure function pair — canReadReview / isRevealedToReviewee — and visibility.test.ts asserts the two AGREE in every combination of (released, submitted, flagged), because two functions expressing one rule is precisely how the filter and the check come to differ. The reviewer can always re-read their own review, released or not and flagged or not: it is their own writing, withholding it would be theatre, and a student told their review was withheld must be able to see what they wrote.

[2026-07-31 19:05] test(peer-review): the authorization NEGATIVES, at three levels, because each one can only see part of it — why: an authorization bug here does not throw, it shows one student another student's feedback. 19 pure tests enumerate the refusals as a table (stranger reading a review; reviewee reading before release; reviewee reading a flagged review; the REVIEWEE trying to write the review of their own work, who has the most motive of anyone; editing a submitted review; editing a flagged one). Then 16 INTEGRATION tests against real Postgres, because the other half of the story is SQL predicates a mocked client cannot execute — reviews.anonymity.test.ts says so in its own footer rather than implying coverage it does not have. Those 16 prove: Postgres REFUSES a self-review allocation (and the same INSERT SUCCEEDS on a scratch table without the CHECK, so the test shows its own sensitivity — the standard the queue and badges streams set); both unique indexes are UNIQUE read from pg_indexes, since a plain index satisfies every query in this codebase; FOUR simultaneous submits of one review through the real submitReview leave ONE row with exactly one caller told ok and the rest `already_submitted`, which is what makes "cannot be edited" true against a double-clicked Save and not merely against a second sequential attempt; a stranger gets not_found from getReviewTask, submitReview and getReceivedReviews with nothing written; the reveal gate holds in SQL both before and after release; and a flagged review is withheld while its author can still read it. Fixtures are one probe course/week/assignment plus three prefixed users with cohort_id NULL, deleted in afterAll by prefix as well as by id — verified afterwards that zero rows and zero scratch tables were left in the shared database. One assertion of mine FAILED first and was wrong rather than the code: `expect(serialised).not.toContain(String(reviewId))` with reviewId = 1, because "1" occurs inside a legitimate maxPoints: 15 and inside a timestamp — a substring search for a small integer is a coin flip, so it was replaced with a structural check over key NAMES plus an exact pin of the revealed review's key set.

[2026-07-31 19:05] feat(peer-review): peer review awards NO marks, so an empty review cannot profit — why: this is the interaction with the frozen scoring contract and it is the whole answer to the gaming question. src/lib/contracts/scoring.ts#assignmentPoints was changed TODAY to return 0 for `stars: null`, and its note explains that the previous optimistic default meant merely INGESTING a Google Form response awarded the full 40 and the grade went DOWN when a human marked it — "the one direction a grade must never move for a reason the student cannot see". A peer-review score reaching submissions.score would undo exactly that: a second path awarding assignment points, and the optimistic one again, derived from two classmates on work no member of staff had opened. The syllabus breakdown has four buckets and none of them is a classmate's opinion. So this stream writes NOTHING to submissions, attendance, progress or leaderboard and fires NO ScoringEvent — there is no second path that can award points, and nothing for the badges fan-out riding on-scoring-event.ts to react to. total_score on peer_reviews is advisory and labelled so in the schema; the student page says in writing that peer feedback does not change marks. PARTICIPATION stays the instructor's to award through attendance.participation_score (POINTS.PARTICIPATION_MAX = 10), which the round overview gives them the evidence for. SECONDARY DEFENCES, since "no points" does not stop a student wasting a classmate's reveal: a 120-character floor on the TRIMMED string plus every rubric criterion scored, both refusals at submit and not warnings; the human release gate; and flagging, which withholds a review from its reviewee while listing it WITH its author's name for the instructor — the "an instructor can see and override" answer, stated as such. NOT DEFENDED, and said plainly rather than dressed up: 150 characters of fluent nonsense passes every automated check, and no automated check can catch that. NOT BUILT: the peer_review_master badge, though src/lib/badges/catalogue.ts:23-24 left it out only because no peer_reviews table existed — it does now, the fact it needs is exported as countSubmittedReviewsByReviewer, and the decision belongs to the badges owner because a review completion is not a ScoringEvent.

[2026-07-31 19:05] feat(peer-review): reviewer notifications go through the existing producer, reusing the course_message type rather than adding an enum label — why: the brief said use src/lib/notifications/** and not a new mechanism, and that stream's own header says a producer belongs in producers.ts "so that 'what can enqueue work' is answerable by reading one screen" — so notifyPeerReviewAssigned lives there, in the same shape as the other three, with only the WORDING in this stream as a pure leaf (notify-text.ts) that has no database or mail imports. NO NEW notification_type LABEL: adding one means ALTER TYPE plus a notification_preferences column plus entries in two exhaustive maps and PREFERENCE_DEFAULTS/fromRow, and the wave generates exactly ONE migration whose _journal.json was corrupted twice today by concurrent generation. course_message is labelled "Message from an instructor" and a peer-review assignment IS an instructor act. THE COST IS STATED: a student switching off "Message from an instructor" also switches off peer-review notices, and the two cannot be separated until the label exists — TODO left at the key. THE KEY SCOPING LESSON APPLIED, which is why this needed its own minter: every notification shares the single `notification_email` kind, so the TYPE segment is what distinguishes events — and two events of the SAME type from different tables collide, because independent serial sequences mint the same id. peerReviewAssignedKey therefore carries an extra `peer_review` segment (notification_email:course_message:peer_review:41); without it, allocation 41 and a future course-message subject 41 mint one key, the unique index fires, and the second student silently never hears. Keyed on an insert-once allocation/submission id and NOT on the round, because keying on the round means a student allocated more work by a later reconcile is never told. One message per reviewer per reconcile, and a reviewer who gains nothing is not messaged — which is what makes re-running allocation safe. Notification is after the commit and cannot fail the allocation, the same contract applyGrade uses for its enqueue.

[2026-07-31 19:05] chore(peer-review): pages shipped WITHOUT a nav row, a loading.tsx or a loading-shape rule — why: routing files are the coordinator's (brief), and this feature's index page cannot simply take a boundary. /peer-review/[allocationId] calls notFound() for an allocation that is not the caller's, and boundary-scope.test.ts fails any 404-capable route with a Suspense boundary above it and no guard layout at or above that depth — a loading.tsx flushes the 200 status line before notFound() can set 404. The index page is therefore in an `(index)` ROUTE GROUP (the validated pattern from /assignments, /weeks, /problems), so a boundary dropped beside it covers only that route and not its dynamic sibling. WHAT THE COORDINATOR NEEDS TO ADD IF /peer-review JOINS NAV_LINKS, because two shared tests go red without both: (a) src/app/(app)/peer-review/(index)/loading.tsx, two lines delegating to PageSkeleton — boundary-scope.test.ts requires every sidebar destination to have one; (b) `{ prefix: "/peer-review", shape: "list", count: 3 }` in src/lib/navigation/loading-shape.ts, since loading-shape.test.ts requires every NAV_LINKS href to resolve to something other than the fallback. Suggested rows: student "Peer review" -> /peer-review, instructor "Peer review" -> /instructor/peer-review. The instructor page deliberately never calls notFound() — (staff)/instructor/loading.tsx is a boundary at depth 2 of its chain with no guard layout, so an unknown round renders as an empty list instead. ALSO FLAGGED: /peer-review is not in src/middleware.ts's PROTECTED table, so the page's own requireUser() is the only gate — the same gap /assignments has and documents; `{ prefix: "/peer-review", required: "student" }` belongs there as a fast edge reject.

[2026-07-31 19:05] chore(peer-review): mutations are server actions, not routes, and the frozen contract is untouched — why: ROUTES in src/lib/contracts/api.ts grants this stream nothing, and src/lib/instructor/actions.ts already recorded the consequence of inventing a path anyway — "a path with no entry in ROUTE_AUTH, precisely the unguarded-by-omission bug that map exists to prevent". Adding four routes to a frozen file every other stream is editing at the tail of an eight-agent wave is also four more merge points, so nothing in src/lib/contracts/** was modified. All four mutations are server actions whose FIRST statement is a guard (requireUser for the student one, requireRole("instructor") for open/allocate/release/flag), and the student action takes no reviewerId — the session id is the only identity that reaches the query, because a parameter is something a client can change. The read models are three separate functions for the three audiences rather than one with a role flag: a boolean that defaults wrong, or a caller that forgets it, is how a student ends up reading the instructor's view, and only ONE of the three projections carries a reviewer's name.

[2026-07-31 19:15] feat(db): ONE migration for the whole add-on wave (0005_mature_living_mummy) — why: eight feature agents were each forbidden from running db:generate, because drizzle-kit snapshots the ENTIRE schema rather than just the caller's tables — two agents earlier today each produced a 0004 containing the other's tables and a conflicting _journal.json. Generating once, after every schema module was final, produced 14 tables in a single file. AUDITED BEFORE APPLYING rather than trusted: zero DROP/TRUNCATE/DELETE statements, all 34 ALTER TABLEs are ADD CONSTRAINT, and the two constructs most likely to be mis-rendered both came out correct — the activity stream's BRIN index kept its option (`USING brin ("occurred_at") WITH (pages_per_range=32)`, which matters because its own probe measured 24 kB against 8792 kB for a btree) and both partial unique indexes kept their WHERE predicates.
[2026-07-31 19:15] fix(notifications): drop the `export` on NOTIFICATIONS_PATH — why: a "use server" module may only export async functions, since every export becomes a callable server-action endpoint and a string has no call signature. `next build` refused it; `tsc` accepted it, and so did 2801 unit tests, so this survived every check except the production build. Nothing outside the file referenced it.
[2026-07-31 19:15] fix(forums): read ROLES_SATISFYING directly instead of importing roleSatisfies from guard.ts — why: PostComposer.tsx is a CLIENT component and imports forums/policy.ts for two character limits, so the chain policy -> guard -> auth -> src/db -> pg pulled the Postgres driver into the browser bundle and `next build` failed with "Can't resolve 'fs' / 'dns' / 'net'". Neither tsc nor vitest can see this, because both resolve node built-ins happily. The policy module's own header claimed it was pure and it was not. roleSatisfies is four lines over a frozen lookup table, so reading the table directly costs nothing and the two cannot disagree about who counts as staff. Same hazard nav-links.ts documents at its head, where a type-only import of schema.ts keeps Drizzle out of the client.

[2026-08-01 06:30] fix(env): AUTH_SECRET, NEXTAUTH_URL and CRON_SECRET appended to .env; NO SOURCE FILE CHANGED — why: the round-3 "regressions" were not regressions. .env was absent from the working tree (only .env.example present), so src/db/index.ts:39 threw at module load and EVERY database-backed page returned HTTP 500 — measured directly: GET /register and GET /login both status=500, 1534 bytes. That is upstream of both defects under investigation. The account failure was NOT at :129 (the profile save) but at :61, because register() cannot fill input[name="name"] on a 500 error page, and mode:"serial" then aborted the remaining 10 tests — one environment fault reported as eleven. coding-problems' 15 failures were the same 500. The suspects being read at the time — FormNotice's null return, settings/loading.tsx, resolveNotice's "saved" code, problems/[slug]/layout.tsx's notFound() guard, loadProblem's level ladder — were all innocent, and none was reproduced before being suspected. After DATABASE_URL was restored the suite got WORSE (48 failed, 2 passed) for a second missing-variable reason: AUTH_SECRET was still unset, so Auth.js threw MissingSecret 51 times in one dev-server log, which surfaced as 503 on API routes and as loginAs() hitting an error overlay in tests/e2e/fixtures.ts:91 — again read as feature bugs. THE LESSON, and it cost two full sessions: a missing environment variable presents as a large number of failures in whichever feature the suite happens to touch first, and it is indistinguishable from a code regression in the report. Diagnosing by reading code paths cannot find it; one curl against the running server finds it in a second. NEXTAUTH_URL is set to http://127.0.0.1:3000 and not localhost, to match the BASE_URL in playwright.config.ts — a host mismatch between the two is a cookie-domain mismatch, i.e. a third way to produce authentication failures that look like feature bugs. .env.example documents all four variables; only DATABASE_URL had been filled in. STILL OPEN for the devops-testing stream, which owns the harness: nothing fails FAST on an incomplete environment. A precheck that asserts the four names before the first browser launches, and aborts the run rather than letting 500s masquerade as assertions, would have replaced both of these sessions with one line of output. | feature/perf-and-subject-sections

[2026-08-01 06:30] chore(test): Playwright browser binaries installed; package-lock.json reverted — why: two harness faults, neither in application code. (1) chromium_headless_shell-1234 did not exist on disk, so global-setup.ts:134 threw browserType.launch before a single assertion ran — the whole suite was unrunnable on this machine regardless of the environment problem above. Chromium (Chrome for Testing 151.0.7922.34), Firefox 153.0 and WebKit 26.5 are now present; the config's two projects (chromium, mobile-chrome) both need chromium. (2) The working tree's only modification was package-lock.json: 1 insertion, 153 deletions, every one of them a `libc: ["glibc"]` field being dropped from an optional platform dependency. That is an older npm rewriting the lockfile, carries no dependency change, and would churn CI, so it was reverted rather than committed. Also removed the .next directory before the CI run: playwright.config.ts's webServer comment records that `next dev` and `next start` share it and that a dev build served as production returns 400 for every hashed chunk, which fails only the tests needing interactivity and looks exactly like a hydration bug. | feature/perf-and-subject-sections

[2026-08-01 07:05] fix(test): webServer.timeout is now conditional — 900 s under CI, 180 s otherwise (playwright.config.ts) — why: `command` on that block is two different things depending on CI, and it had ONE wait for both. Without CI it waits for `next dev` to bind a port, which takes about 7 s here and for which 180 s was always generous. With CI it waits for `next build && next start`, i.e. a full production build FIRST — measured 47 s to compile, plus static generation and build traces on a cold cache — and that overran 180 s. The failure mode is the misleading part and the reason this is worth a log entry: the build SUCCEEDED, the log shows "✓ Generating static pages (8/8)" and then "Collecting build traces ...", and Playwright killed it mid-trace with "Error: Timed out waiting 180000ms from config.webServer." Nothing in that message contains the word "build", so it reads as the server failing to start, which is the third time in two sessions that an infrastructure fault has presented as something else entirely. "⚠ No build cache found" is the normal state of a fresh CI runner, so the COLD path is the one the budget has to fit — a value tuned to a warm local rebuild fails on every clean CI checkout. Deliberately not solved by pre-building outside Playwright and pointing it at an already-running server: playwright.config.ts's own webServer comment records that hand-starting a server and letting a second command touch .next is how a dev build ends up served as production, returning 400 for every hashed chunk. Letting Playwright own both halves keeps the build and the server from disagreeing. | feature/perf-and-subject-sections

[2026-08-01 07:35] feat(env): src/lib/env.ts + src/instrumentation.ts — the server now REFUSES TO BOOT on an incomplete environment, naming every missing variable at once — why: this is the "bursts in production" case, and it is not hypothetical — it happened twice in two sessions, each time costing hours and each time misattributed to a feature. The production shape is the dangerous one: `next build` does not need AUTH_SECRET and NO FILE UNDER src/** REFERENCES IT (Auth.js reads process.env itself), so a Vercel deployment missing it BUILDS GREEN, DEPLOYS GREEN, serves its public pages, and then 500s the instant a student signs in — with Auth.js's MissingSecret in a log nobody is reading. The failure reaches a student before it reaches whoever deployed it. register() in instrumentation.ts runs once per server start / serverless cold start, which is the earliest application code runs, so the check happens there and not per request; a per-request check reports the same misconfiguration hundreds of times and reports it against whichever page was requested first. DATABASE_URL's existing guard at src/db/index.ts:36 is deliberately LEFT WHERE IT IS rather than moved, because it also protects scripts/** which import { db } without the server bootstrap; env.ts is the AGGREGATE check, which matters because fixing them one deploy at a time is the same trap in slow motion. TWO TIERS, and the split is a judgement worth recording: DATABASE_URL and AUTH_SECRET are FATAL (no authenticated request can succeed); CRON_SECRET and NEXTAUTH_URL only WARN, because refusing to boot the entire platform to protect one scheduled job is the wrong trade and requireCron already fails those routes closed with 503. A PLACEHOLDER COUNTS AS MISSING (/replace-with|your-|changeme|xxxx/) and values are trimmed before the empty check, because the actual mistake made here was copying .env.example and filling in only DATABASE_URL — a truthiness test on the raw value passes both a placeholder and a stray " " straight through to Auth.js. Secrets are also length-checked at 32, matching what .env.example asks for; the check is on length not entropy, which cannot be measured from one sample. The edge runtime is skipped (NEXT_RUNTIME guard) because src/middleware.ts runs where process.env carries only build-time inlines, so a check there would report a false absence. 12 unit tests in src/lib/env.test.ts, all passing, asserting the aggregate behaviour specifically — that a fully-cleared environment yields TWO named problems and not one. | feature/perf-and-subject-sections

[2026-08-01 07:35] fix(test): global-setup.ts prechecks the environment and ASSERTS GET /login returns 200 before any spec runs — why: two independent guards against the same class of wasted session, and the second is the cheaper one. (1) The env precheck runs before a browser is even launched and THROWS, which aborts the entire run. That is deliberate: a suite executing against a broken environment does not produce a weaker signal than one that refuses to run, it produces a WRONG one, and a wrong signal costs more than no signal because it gets acted upon — two sessions spent reading FormNotice, settings/loading.tsx, resolveNotice, problems/[slug]/layout.tsx and loadProblem's level ladder, all five innocent, is the evidence. (2) The startup probe now checks the HTTP STATUS instead of merely arriving. page.goto RESOLVES HAPPILY ON A 500 — that one fact is the whole misdirection: every subsequent page.fill then times out waiting for an input a Next.js error page does not contain, and the report reads "waiting for locator('input[name=\"name\"]')" at account.spec.ts:61 while /register was quietly serving status 500. Measured: curl showed status=500 in under a second, against ~30 minutes of reading application code. The status check is kept SEPARATE from the env precheck because it also catches the faults no variable explains — an unapplied migration, a dev build being served as production (the 400-on-every-hashed-chunk hazard playwright.config.ts's webServer header documents), or a crash on boot. Warnings are printed rather than thrown for the recommended variables, and are worth printing loudly: an unset CRON_SECRET makes nine specs across submissions, queue and grand-quiz assert against a 503 they will read as a feature failure, which is the state playwright.config.ts's header records as previously being hidden by test.skip. | feature/perf-and-subject-sections

[2026-08-01 07:55] feat(ui): root error.tsx, global-error.tsx and not-found.tsx — the app had ONE error boundary and no 404 page — why: audited for what takes the platform down in front of a student rather than in a test report, and this was the largest gap. Before these three files, src/app/(app)/practice/error.tsx was the ONLY error boundary in the entire application, so an unexpected throw anywhere else — a failed query on the dashboard, the leaderboard, a lecture page, the grading queue — fell through to Next.js's built-in error page: unstyled, unbranded, and with NO ROUTE BACK INTO THE APP. The audience here is students who cannot read a stack trace and cannot redeploy, so a dead end IS the failure. THE 404 IS THE MORE IMPORTANT OF THE THREE, and this is the non-obvious part: notFound() is the primary AUTHORIZATION outcome across several streams, not merely the answer to a mistyped URL — a peer-review allocation that is not the caller's, a coding problem below the caller's level, a locked week, an instructor route reached by a student. Those streams chose 404 over 403 deliberately so a probe cannot learn whether a row exists (argued in the peer-review headers). Every one of them was landing on the built-in page, which means the most common outcome of the platform's own authorization rules was a dead end. The new page therefore gives NO SPECIFIC REASON — "you do not have access to this submission" would hand back exactly the fact the 404 exists to withhold — and offers both /dashboard and /login, because it is reachable signed out. WHY BOTH error.tsx AND global-error.tsx, since one file looks sufficient: a segment error.tsx renders INSIDE the root layout and so cannot catch an error thrown BY that layout. global-error.tsx REPLACES the root layout, which is why it emits its own <html>/<body> and why its styles are INLINE — globals.css is imported by the very layout it is standing in for, so token class names would resolve to nothing there. That is deliberate, not an oversight, and is the one place in this repo where inline styles are correct. Neither renders error.message or a stack: production Next.js withholds those from the client because they carry file paths and query fragments, and re-adding them would undo that — `digest` is shown instead, which is the same value the server logs, so a student's screenshot is enough to find the entry. /practice keeps its own boundary (React uses the closest one) because it degrades the practice area alone and says so in its copy. No Suspense interaction: an error boundary does not buffer the response, so unlike a stray loading.tsx it cannot swallow a 404 status — verified, boundary-scope.test.ts and loading-shape.test.ts still pass (30/30 with cross-stream-contracts). typecheck and eslint clean. | feature/perf-and-subject-sections

