which is the only convincing demonstration of that method. ACCESSIBILITY, the part most likely to be done badly, and the specific choices: (1) NATIVE `<input type="range">` and `<select>`, never a div with role="slider" — the native controls already implement arrow keys, Home/End, the value announcement and switch-device support, and reimplementing that by hand is how drag-only interactions ship; an EXPLICIT keydown handler is layered on top anyway for two reasons, that jsdom implements no key handling on a range so the keyboard contract would otherwise be UNTESTABLE, and that it adds PageUp/PageDown so dragging padding 0->100 is not 100 keypresses. (2) NOTHING IS ENCODED IN COLOUR ALONE (WCAG 1.4.1): the box model's four layers are told apart by THREE redundant cues — a text label in the legend, a distinct border style (dashed/double/dotted/solid) and a distinct diagonal hatch — so the diagram survives greyscale; the HTTP stage marked current carries the literal word "current" plus aria-current="step"; the spanned grid item says "spans 2x1" in text; the winning selector says "wins". (3) A POLITE LIVE REGION per component, because a sighted user SEES the box resize and a screen-reader user is told nothing otherwise — polite and not assertive, since a slider sweep generates one change per keypress and assertive would interrupt itself into noise. (4) REDUCED MOTION DEGRADES TO A STATIC EQUIVALENT and never to a faster animation, following the rule already stated in src/lib/exercises/reduced-motion.ts: the HTTP diagram hides autoplay entirely and says why, the propagation log renders complete at once instead of revealing step by step, and every stage title and log entry is in the DOM in BOTH modes. (5) The container elements of the flexbox and grid playgrounds are aria-hidden with the code panel and the summary line as the accessible equivalent — read aloud, "1 2 3 4 5" conveys nothing about alignment and the declaration conveys everything. TYPING: the spec declared `o