ing the reader. LIFECYCLE IS THE HARD PART OF WRAPPING REVEAL AND IT IS ASSERTED, NOT ASSUMED: Reveal mutates the DOM React owns, binds document-level key handlers and installs a resize observer, so an instance created and never destroyed keeps all of that alive after React discards the nodes. The symptom is not a crash — it is the slide editor's live preview, which remounts on every keystroke, accumulating handlers until one arrow press advances nine decks. Every creation path has exactly one destruction path, initialize() is guarded by a `disposed` flag because React StrictMode unmounts mid-promise on every development mount, and RevealDeck.test.tsx asserts destroy() fires once per mount across three remounts. The factory and the onSlideChange callback are both held in REFS rather than effect dependencies: a caller writing `createReveal={() => x}` inline passes a new identity every render, and an effect depending on it would rebuild the engine on every parent render, losing the viewer's position mid-talk. That was a real defect found by the sync test, not a hypothetical. SSR: RevealDeck statically imports reveal.js, which reads `document`, so it is reached ONLY through RevealPresentation's next/dynamic({ ssr: false }) and is deliberately NOT re-exported from the barrel — exporting it is precisely how someone breaks `next build` later. readOnly MODE CREATES NO ENGINE AT ALL rather than creating a disabled one: the thumbnail pane renders one component per slide, and twenty Reveal instances each binding document-level keyboard handlers and each claiming to be "the" presentation is the failure that mode exists to avoid. A static render is also inert by construction — there is no key handler left to accidentally re-enable. THEMING IS OURS, NOT REVEAL'S: reveal.js ships themes as full stylesheets that set colour, type and layout together at global element scope, which would both make decks look like generic Reveal and fight the Tailwind layer. Only dist/reveal.css (structure) is imported; appearance comes from --rp-* custom properties derived from appConfig.branding.colors, so re-branding the app re-skins every deck. Foreground is CHOSEN by WCAG relative luminance rather than hardcoded white, because a pale accent as a slide background is one colour-picker click away and slide text failing contrast on a projector is the most common accessibility complaint about decks. PDF EXPORT IS REAL, NOT A PRINT PROMPT, and the reasoning is in the file header: Reveal's ?print-pdf route cannot be CALLED, only offered — it needs a routed URL and the browser's own print dialog. @react-pdf/renderer was already a dependency (certificates) and produces a Blob in the browser with no server and no dialog, so each slide variant is re-expressed in react-pdf's primitives. The honest cost, stated rather than hidden: the result is a document rendering of the deck, not a pixel copy of the projected slides — for the handout students actually ask for that is the better artefact, and the pixel-faithful case is served by the HTML export's one-slide-per-page print stylesheet plus PRINT_TO_PDF_INSTRUCTIONS. The slide-to-page translation is a PURE function so it is unit-tested; the final Blob generation is not, because @react-pdf/renderer cannot run under jsdom. THE HTML EXPORT IS NOT A REVEAL DECK, deliberately: shipping a standalone Reveal file means either inlining assets this pure module cannot read or a CDN <script>, and a "standalone" file that renders blank offline is worse than not offering one. It emits a semantic document — no script, no link, no external anything — which the tests assert. Slide bodies render through JSX in the app (React escapes everything, no dangerouslySetInnerHTML anywhere) and through an explicit escaping serializer in the export. ACCESSIBILITY: a polite live region announces "Slide 3 of 12: <title>" and is a SIBLING of the deck rather than inside it, because Reveal aria-hides non-current slides and an announcement placed in the sta