[2026-08-01 13:55] feat(realtime): services/realtime — a standalone Socket.io service, plus the HMAC handshake token that lets it trust an identity it cannot read — why: the service runs on a host OUTSIDE Vercel because a serverless function is frozen when its response is flushed, so there is nothing for a WebSocket to stay open inside. That one fact drives every decision here. It CANNOT READ THE AUTH.JS SESSION COOKIE — the cookie is scoped to the app's origin, the socket connects cross-origin, and the service has neither AUTH_SECRET's session decryption nor the sessions table — so identity travels with the handshake as an HMAC-SHA256 token minted by the Next app (src/lib/live-classes/realtime-token.ts). THE CLIENT NEVER NAMES ITS OWN IDENTITY, and that is the entire security model: the app, which does hold the session, signs the decision, and a student who edits `role` to `instructor` invalidates the MAC at the handshake. No JWT library, on either side — a JWT buys `alg` negotiation and JWKS rotation, neither of which applies to a symmetric secret shared between two processes the same team deploys, while bringing algorithm-confusion with it; node:crypto is already in both runtimes. 120 s TTL because the token covers "page rendered" to "socket connected" and nothing else. timingSafeEqual is length-guarded because it THROWS on a length mismatch, which would turn a rejection into a 500. Verification order is shape, then signature, then EXPIRY LAST, so the timestamp being compared is one we signed rather than attacker-controlled bytes. THE VERIFIER IS DUPLICATED into the service (services/realtime/src/auth/token.ts) rather than imported or extracted to a workspace package: the Dockerfile copies ONLY services/realtime, which is what makes the image deploy unchanged to Railway, Fly or Render, and a relative import out of the package is a build that works on a laptop and fails on every host; a workspace would mean a root package.json change that eight parallel streams would all have to rebase onto, for 120 dependency-free lines. The duplication is pinned by a CONTRACT TEST holding a literal token minted by the app's own minter, so drift fails a test instead of rejecting every handshake in production. AUTHORIZATION IS A TABLE, NOT AN `if` PER HANDLER, because a per-handler check is invisible when it is MISSING — a new event with no entry is a boot failure via assertEveryEventIsGoverned rather than a default-allow, and chat:pin / qa:answer / qa:pin / qa:resolve are refused server-side rather than merely hidden in the UI. Dispatch order is RATE LIMIT, then AUTHORIZE, then VALIDATE: parsing an attacker's payload is work, so limiting first makes a flood of garbage cost them something, and authorizing before validating means a student emitting chat:pin is told "forbidden" rather than "invalid_payload", which would leak the schema to someone with no right to it. THE RATE LIMITER IS KEYED ON BOTH SOCKET AND USER because per-socket alone is bypassed by opening a second tab — one keystroke of effort — and both buckets are evaluated before either is charged, so a user at their limit does not also drain their per-tab budget and get throttled twice as long as intended. Capacity 10 against a 5/s refill: a capacity equal to the rate fires on a student pasting three lines, which teaches users the app is broken. Token bucket rather than a fixed window because a fixed 1 s window admits 10 events across a boundary — double the intended rate at exactly the moment a burst happens. NO TIMER ANYWHERE EXCEPT server.ts, and there are exactly two, both cleared in close(): typing indicators carry an expiry timestamp swept centrally rather than a setTimeout per keystroke (a timer allocated on a client-controlled path, which also leaks if the socket vanishes in between), and connected time is computed from a stored connectedSince rather than ticked. Engagement is IN MEMORY and flushed on disconnect and shutdown — a chat message already costs one INSERT and incrementing a counter row on the same path would double the write rate of the busiest thing the service does, to produce a number nobody reads until the class is over; the loss window is acceptable ONLY because the underlying chat and Q&A rows are already durable, so the statistic is recomputable. Multi-socket aware in both places: two tabs is one presence and one attendance clock, or a student doubles their attendance term by opening a second tab. THE ENGAGEMENT FORMULA, recorded because a scoring rule nobody can find is a scoring rule nobody can challenge: min(100, 4*messages + 8*questions + 8*answers + 2*upvotes + 1*reactions + 30*min(1, connectedMinutes/45)). Attendance is the only saturating term and is worth 30 — a student who attends the whole class and says nothing scores 30, because silence in a lecture is not disengagement and a formula that scores it zero is measuring extroversion. The cap is load-bearing: without it the metric rewards volume without limit and the top of any ranking is whoever typed most. THE LOW-ENGAGEMENT ALERT IS OFF BY DEFAULT and its threshold is 15, deliberately BELOW the 30 that mere attendance earns, so it cannot fire for a quiet student — it fires for one who was barely connected; a punitive automatic message about a student's participation derived from a proxy metric is a pastoral decision, not one a socket server makes because a brief used the word "notify". Persistence sits behind ChatStore/QaStore/EngagementStore with a real in-memory implementation that enforces the SAME invariants as the SQL (ownership, one upvote per user, soft delete that clears the body, idempotent reactions), because a permissive fake lets an authorization bug pass every test; the live-classes schema is being written concurrently so pg.ts names every expected column beside a TODO(schema) and is the only file in the service that knows a column name. A MODERATOR MAY DELETE BUT MAY NOT EDIT somebody else's message — putting words in a student's mouth under their own name is a different act from removing them, and no moderation need requires it. Root tsconfig.json and eslint.config.mjs now exclude services/**: the root include glob is `**/*.ts` and the root exclude of `node_modules` does not cover a NESTED one, so `tsc --noEmit` would otherwise compile the service under the Next app's moduleResolution and fail on imports that resolve perfectly where they actually run. MEASURED, not asserted from theory: 12 paced samples of chat:send to a peer's chat:message over loopback websocket with the in-memory store gave a median of 4.0 ms and a p95 of 5.3 ms — reported rather than checked against the brief's 500 ms, because a loopback number says nothing about a student on hotel wifi and the only honest claim it supports is that the service itself is not the cost. The first version of that test failed by sending 20 messages as fast as the loop allowed, exhausting the token bucket and timing out on a broadcast the server had correctly refused — the limiter working, presenting as a delivery fault. 120 tests pass in the service, 39 in src/lib/live-classes. | feature/lms-complete-enhancement

