tion path here would be a second place to get that wrong. It returns `persisted: false` in the payload so no client builds a progress metric on the assumption that it did. Every list endpoint routes through one parser with a documented MAX_PAGE_SIZE of 100, clamping rather than rejecting an over-large limit because a client asking for 5000 rows means "as many as you'll give me". Denormalized counters (assignments.samples_count, lectures.practice_problems_count, lectures.visualizations_count) are maintained by these handlers INSIDE the same transaction as the row that changes them, as relative `+ 1` / `greatest(- 1, 0)` SQL fragments rather than read-modify-write — two concurrent handlers reading 4 and both writing 5 is the standard way a denormalized counter loses an increment, and the greatest() floor stops a double-fired decrement from aborting the DELETE it rides along with via the >= 0 CHECK. Constraint violations are classified by SQLSTATE (23505/23503/23514) into 409/422 rather than surfacing as 500s: the whole point of choosing a UNIQUE index over a read-then-write check is that it has no race, and that only pays off if the caller is told "somebody took position 3" instead of "we broke". execution_mode was corrected from the spec's "server" to the enum's actual "piston" — a parallel spelling is a value Postgres rejects at INSERT with an error naming neither the field nor the allowed set. | feature/lms-complete-enhancement
[2026-08-01 07:10] feat(api): live-classes routes (schedule, lifecycle, join/leave, attendance, chat, Q&A, recordings) — why: SUBAGENT 2's second increment. 23 handlers across 11 route files behind featureGate("liveClasses"). EVERY READ IS AN EXPLICIT select()/join(): src/db/index.ts builds the Drizzle client with `{ schema }` from schema.ts only, so `db.query.liveClasses` is a runtime TypeError — and the technical spec's own example code (LIVE_CLASSES_..._SPEC.md:432) calls exactly that. OWNERSHIP IS A WHERE CLAUSE, NEVER AN `if` AFTER THE FETCH: ownershipFilter(user) returns eq(instructor_id, session.id) for an instructor and undefined for an admin (covering for an absent colleague must not require a role change), and it is ANDed into every class-scoped write, so instructor B editing instructor A's class gets a 404 rather than a 403 that would confirm the class exists. /start and /end are IDEMPOTENT by putting the required prior status in the UPDATE's WHERE clause rather than reading first: a retry matches no row and is answered 200 with the current state. This matters concretely — a second /start that restamped started_at would silently shorten every attendance duration computed from it, and would mint a second Jitsi room stranding the students already in the first. /join uses onConflictDoUpdate against class_attendance's UNIQUE(class_id, student_id) with NO check-then-insert anywhere: two tabs opening at once would both see "no row" and both insert, which is the race the index exists to reject. attendance_count is incremented only on a genuine first insert, detected by comparing the returned joined_at against the instant the request generated — a reconnecting student must not inflate it. /end SWEEPS every attendance row still showing a student present, because /leave fires from a closing tab and a student who shuts the laptop lid never sends it; without the sweep their time_present_minutes stays null and the participation mark treats a full attendee as absent. The swept minutes are floored at 0 (a clock step would otherwise abort the whole transaction via the >= 0 CHECK) and capped at the class duration (a tab left open overnight must not accrue hundreds of minutes into a mark). /leave takes the client's minutesPresent as a HINT and clamps it against wall-clock since joined_at and the class duration, and uses max() not += so a retried sendBeacon cannot double a student's presence. Chat and Q&A share class_chat/class_qa with services/realtime, so the tombstone convention matches its ChatStore contract: soft-deleted rows are RETURNED with message: null rather than filtered, or a reader is left unsure whether they misread the transcript. Chat paginates by a descending keyset on the serial id, not by offset — a transcript grows at the end while you page through it and offset paging silently repeats rows — and an unbounded read of class_chat is a DoS any signed-in student can fire from a URL bar. TWO CROSS-AGENT CONTRACT GAPS RECORDED RATHER THAN PAPERED OVER: (1) the realtime ChatMessage type carries deletedAt and reactions, class_chat has is_deleted and no reactions column at all; the database is the authority and services/realtime/src/store/pg.ts, the adapter that must reconcile them, does not exist yet. (2) QaStore promises "one vote per user", which is NOT IMPLEMENTABLE against class_qa — it has an upvotes integer and no per-user vote table, so there is nowhere to record who voted. /upvote implements a bounded increment (ceiling 500, own-question excluded) and states the cost in its header: a student can repeatedly upvote to promote a question in a queue ordered by upvotes. A class_qa_votes table with UNIQUE(question_id, user_id) fixes it and is flagged to the schema stream. Deleting a class that has recorded attendance is REFUSED with 409, not cascaded: class_attendance, class_chat and class_qa all cascade from live_classes, so the delete would destroy the record a participation mark was computed from. | feature/lms-complete-enhancement

[2026-08-01 08:30] fix(seed): demo users are RE-ASSERTED on every run, not skipped as "exists" (scripts/seed.ts) — why: the seed was NON-CONVERGENT for the one fact every e2e suite depends on. The demo-user loop was `if (!existing) { insert } else { note("exists") }`, so once an existing fixture account's password drifted, `npm run db:seed` reported "exists" and repaired NOTHING — permanently, across every subsequent run. THE INCIDENT: admin@codequeenshub.test (id=2) no longer matched DEMO_PASSWORD. Proven directly rather than inferred — a throwaway probe ran bcrypt.compare("Passw0rd!demo", passwordHash) for all three fixture accounts and got student=true, instructor=true, ADMIN=FALSE. THE COST, and this is why it is worth the long note: 26 failures spread across four suites whose test titles name no admin at all — activity-log (18 of 26 in that file), auth (4, including "the seeded demo accounts all log in"), analytics (2), certificates (2) — plus global-setup's warm-up timing out on the admin login with page.waitForURL: Timeout 120000ms while student and instructor warmed cleanly. That warm-up line was the only honest signal in the entire report and it was one line above 97 misleading ones. This is the SAME failure shape as the two environment faults logged earlier today: one piece of broken setup, reported as many feature bugs, in features nobody had edited. The seed's closing console.log PROMISES "Demo login password for all three accounts: Passw0rd!demo" — printing that while leaving a stale hash in place is asserting something the script has not checked, so re-hashing on every run is what makes its own output true. Role and cohortId are re-asserted for the same reason: a drifted role fails the authorization specs in the identically misleading way, and the seed is the DEFINITION of what these three accounts are. Scoped to the hard-coded DEMO_USERS trio of @codequeenshub.test addresses, so it cannot touch a real student's password — no real address is in that list. Verified: seed now reports "0 created, 3 updated, 46 already present" and the probe returns bcryptMatch=true for all three. A first attempt passed note("reset", …), which is not one of that function's three accepted labels — corrected to "updated", which is also what makes the summary line honest (it had printed "0 updated" while updating three rows). | feature/perf-and-subject-sections
[2026-08-01 07:20] feat(api): presentation routes (decks, slides, theme, export, present, submissions, feedback) — why: SUBAGENT 2's third increment. 18 handlers across 9 route files behind featureGate("presentations"). A DECK IS STUDENT-OWNED WORK, which is why every one of these is ROUTE_AUTH "student" and why the real decision is per ROW rather than per role: authorizing by role alone would let any signed-in student list and read every classmate's unpublished coursework. src/app/api/presentations/_access.ts holds the rule — READ is creator OR staff OR published OR public OR shared-with-role; WRITE is creator only, plus admins — and both are Drizzle WHERE predicates, so the wrong caller matches no row and gets a 404 rather than a 403 that would confirm the deck exists. THE READ/WRITE ASYMMETRY IS DELIBERATE AND IS THE PART WORTH ARGUING ABOUT: staff may read every deck including work in progress (they need to see it to help with it, and to grade a submission whose author forgot to publish) but staff may NOT write a student's, because an instructor rewriting submitted work and leaving it under the student's name is indistinguishable from the student having written it. SPEAKER NOTES ARE PRESENTER-ONLY, enforced by slideColumnsFor() returning TWO projection objects one of which does not name the column — same mechanism as the answer-key barrier, for the same reason: a column never selected cannot be reintroduced by an edit to a response builder. The deck DOCUMENT is a single jsonb blob that a column projection cannot reach into, so stripSpeakerNotes() filters it after the fetch; that is the one unavoidable post-fetch filter in this stream and it is confined to one function so it is visible. THE CANONICAL SLIDE CONTRACT WAS FOUND AND IS IMPORTED, NOT REIMPLEMENTED: src/lib/presentations/types.ts (the Reveal.js stream's discriminated union + zod schema) validates every slide and every deck this API accepts, and its emptyDeck()/renumberSlides()/parseSlideDeck() are used rather than local equivalents — its header says the API stream builds to it, and a second looser shape would defeat the point of the file existing. Stored documents are PARSED, never cast: `as SlideDeck` on a malformed row produces a crash deep in the renderer naming neither the row nor the field, so a bad document is a 422 with the zod field path. slides_json WINS over presentation_slides (DECISIONS.md), so every write goes document-first and rebuilds the projection FROM what was stored; deleting a slide rebuilds all projection rows rather than shifting them, because moving 5 to 4 while 5 still exists collides with presentation_slides_number_idx. PUT /slides/:n overrides the body's slideNumber with the PATH's — the URL addresses the slide, and a disagreeing body would either silently reorder the deck or collide with the unique index. Submission is an onConflictDoUpdate against UNIQUE(assignment_id, student_id), not check-then-insert, and RESUBMITTING CLEARS THE GRADE (score, feedback, graded_by, graded_at all nulled together, status back to submitted) because presentation_submissions_grade_consistent requires a grade to be whole or absent and, more to the point, a score attached to work the instructor has not seen is worse than no score. Grading writes all four grade columns in ONE .set() for the same CHECK, and graded_by comes from the session — an id in the payload would let one instructor record a colleague as the grader of a mark they did not give. Feedback derives feedback_type (self > instructor > peer) and to_user_id from the deck row inside the transaction rather than from the payload, because presentation_feedback_self_typed CHECKs the self case and a client-supplied type would let a student label their own five-star review of their own deck as 'peer'. Export has TWO gates (visibility, then allow_export) and escapes every interpolation, because the exported file is opened from disk with no CSP — escaping there is the only defence, not defence in depth; PDF is NOT offered despite the spec listing it, because PDF here is Reveal's browser print view and advertising a format the server cannot produce is worse than omitting it; requesting speaker notes does not grant them and the response says which way it went rather than silently ignoring the flag. view_count is not incremented for the creator (an author refreshing their editor would otherwise drive the number the gallery presents as audience interest) and presentation_count is deliberately not idempotent (each call counts one delivery, which is what the column answers). VERIFIED AGAINST THE LIVE NEON DATABASE: a throwaway probe executed all 16 non-obvious SQL constructs from this stream — the jsonb @> visibility clause, the slideCount correlated subquery, all four ON CONFLICT targets, the recording_status CASE, the least/greatest attendance expression and the counter fragments — every one compiled and ran, writes inside rolled-back transactions. | feature/lms-complete-enhancement

