[2026-08-01 14:50] fix(live-classes): reconcile the realtime store, the REST routes and the database — one vote ledger, one tombstone convention, one engagement table — why: three streams built against each other's assumptions and two of them disagreed with the schema. services/realtime/src/store/pg.ts wrote SQL against live_class_chat, live_class_qa, live_class_qa_upvotes and live_class_engagement; NONE of those four tables exists. Verified against information_schema on the live Neon database. Every statement in that file carried a TODO(schema) and had never executed — the service's 120 tests all ran on the in-memory store, so the adapter typechecked, passed CI and would have thrown on its first production INSERT. That is the root cause of everything below, and the reason deliverable E is a contract suite rather than more memory-store tests.

  (1) class_qa_votes(question_id, user_id) added, with the PAIR AS THE PRIMARY KEY — why a table and not application code: both other streams independently reported that one-vote-per-user was unimplementable. The REST route shipped a bounded increment with a ceiling of 500 and documented, in its own header, that a student could hold the button down to climb a queue ordered by that number; the store's interface promised idempotence it had no way to deliver. src/db/schema.peer-review.ts §1 is the house precedent: an invariant enforced by a constraint is enforced, one enforced by application code is enforced until two tabs race. No surrogate serial key, uniquely in this module — a vote has no identity, nothing references one, and a serial + UNIQUE would index a number no query mentions while leaving the table technically able to hold the duplicate the index then forbids.

  (2) class_qa.upvotes SURVIVED as a denormalized display hint, maintained in the same transaction as the ledger insert and incremented only when that insert actually inserted — why not count(*): class_qa_class_unanswered_idx is (class_id, is_answered, upvotes) and serves the instructor's continuously polled working queue (SCHEMA_ENHANCEMENT.md:202). A correlated subquery in the ORDER BY takes the one query that must stay index-served off its index. SCHEMA_ENHANCEMENT.md:325 says counters are display hints that must never gate a decision, and this one does not: the ledger's primary key answers "may this user vote?", the counter only orders a list.

  (3) REACTIONS KEPT, as class_chat.reactions jsonb, rather than deleted from the service — why: chat:react is already wired end to end through the service's schemas, handlers, engagement scoring and tests, and the ONLY missing piece was storage. Removing a working feature to match a column that was never added is the wrong direction. jsonb and not a reactions table because a reaction is never queried apart from its message, so a table buys a join on the hottest query in the product (the transcript tail) to enforce a uniqueness rule that jsonb_agg(DISTINCT ...) in one statement already enforces against concurrent double-taps.

  (4) class_chat.deleted_at added BESIDE is_deleted, held in agreement by class_chat_deleted_consistent — why both: the REST read model branches on the boolean and the socket wire type carries the timestamp, and two writers share the table. The CHECK is what stops the socket service from stamping one half. Same shape as the existing is_answered/answered_at pair. Both REST writers (PATCH and DELETE on chat/[messageId]) now set the pair together, with COALESCE so an idempotent second delete does not rewrite when the first happened. class_qa.resolved_at added for the same reason: the service's setResolved had nowhere to write, and "closed" is genuinely not "answered".

  (5) NO live_class_engagement TABLE WAS CREATED. class_attendance already carries messages_sent, questions_asked, screen_share_count, time_present_minutes and participation_score under UNIQUE(class_id, student_id) — that IS the engagement store at exactly the grain a flush writes. EngagementStore.flush now upserts into it ADDITIVELY (x = class_attendance.x + EXCLUDED.x), because a user reconnects several times per class and each disconnect flushes only the delta; a replacing upsert would record the last fragment as the whole class's participation, wrongly and silently. participation_score is the exception and IS replaced — it is a derived 0-100 value, so summing two would breach the table's own CHECK. COST STATED PLAINLY: answers_given, upvotes_cast and reactions_added have no column and their individual totals are not persisted. They are folded into the score, which is stored, and are recomputable from class_qa, class_qa_votes and the reactions jsonb, all durable. Three integers nothing reads separately did not justify a fifth table.

  (6) Migration 0007_faithful_dragon_lord.sql GENERATED AND NOT APPLIED. Read in full: three ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN, one CREATE TABLE, one index, three CHECKs. NO DROP of any table or column. `npm run db:migrate` was NOT run — the previous migration went to the user's live shared Neon database and a second one is not this agent's call. CONSEQUENCE, so nobody is surprised: until it is applied, the rewritten pg store will fail against production, because deleted_at, reactions, resolved_at and class_qa_votes do not exist there yet. The service defaults to the memory store when DATABASE_URL is absent, so this is a deployment gate rather than an outage.

  (7) Tests: services/realtime/src/store/contract.ts holds the assertions once and contract.test.ts runs them against BOTH stores. Includes the two regression tests the defect needed — the same user upvoting twice increments exactly once, and engagement flushed across two disconnects sums rather than replaces. The pg half requires REALTIME_TEST_DATABASE_URL and is a NAMED, LOUD skip without it, deliberately not reading DATABASE_URL: that variable points at the shared team database and this suite INSERTs and DELETEs. NOT VERIFIED IN THIS SESSION — no local Postgres credentials and no Docker on this machine, so the pg contract suite has never executed. The pg adapter's SQL is therefore reviewed and column-verified against information_schema, not run. That is the same class of gap that produced the original defect and is flagged rather than glossed.

  Verification: tsc clean (root and service); eslint 0 errors; root vitest 3141 passed / 1 pre-existing failure (src/lib/problems/availability.test.ts, present on the base branch); realtime 135 passed + 1 named skip, up from 120. `npm run build` FAILS, and fails identically with these changes stashed — a concurrent agent's src/app/%5Fui/page.tsx breaks prerendering of "/". Proven by building the tree twice, with and without this diff. Not fixed here: it is outside this stream's blast radius.

[2026-08-01 08:10] feat(components): the data-bound surfaces for the add-on wave — learning enhancements, live classes, the presentation builder, and the realtime client — why, decision by decision:

  (1) src/lib/client/api.ts + use-api-resource.ts — the first CLIENT consumer of the frozen ROUTES map. Every component in this wave builds its URLs with apiPath("GET  /api/classes/:classId", {classId}), typed against keyof typeof ROUTES, so a renamed route is a compile error instead of a 404 in a student's browser at class time. Nothing in the repo did this before: every existing client writes fetch("/api/quizzes/" + id + "/submit"). apiRequest returns a discriminated VALUE rather than throwing, because the panels have to tell "409 chat is disabled" apart from "the network died" and a catch block cannot. It handles the four real failure shapes, one of which is specific to this codebase: a feature-flagged-off route returns Next's own HTML 404 page, .json() rejects, and without the fallback the panel renders the word "undefined" at a student. useApiResource holds ONE discriminated state, not three booleans, because {loading, error, data} as independent state can express all three at once and that contradiction is the flash-of-stale-error every list eventually grows.

  (2) ProgressiveHintRevealer DIVERGES FROM THE SPEC'S PROP API ON PURPOSE, and this is the most consequential decision in the wave. TECHNICAL_SPECIFICATION.md:816 types it {hints: Array<{level,text}>} — the whole ladder handed in, revealed by a client-side counter. That is not a hint system, it is a progress bar over data the student already holds: hint 3 is in the React props from first paint, one devtools tab away from the person trying not to look. The API stream reached the same conclusion from the other side and BUILT AGAINST IT — /hints?upTo=n returns levels 1..n and the detail payload carries only hintCount and maxHintLevel. So the component takes a problemId and each rung is a REQUEST. The spec's hints prop survives as an instructor-preview escape hatch, and which mode it is in is exposed as data-source so a test can assert the student path is the metered one. The tests assert on the FETCH SPY's arguments and on the ABSENCE of level-3 text from the DOM — a component that fetched all ten and revealed them one at a time would pass a visibility test and fail these.

  (3) PracticeProblemCard's showSolution means "offer the reveal", not "render the answer". It cannot mean the latter: practiceProblemDetailColumns does not select the solution columns, deliberately, and the projection module says why. Pressing it issues the separate GET, which is the entire access-control model that route chose. No UI here suggests the reveal is recorded, because it is not — there is no attempts ledger for these problems and the endpoint writes nothing.

  (4) THE REALTIME HOOK'S DEGRADED PATH IS THE ONLY PATH IN THIS REPOSITORY, and that is a finding, not a shortcut. TWO pieces are missing and NEITHER is in this stream's blast radius: socket.io-client is not in package.json (and writing import("socket.io-client") anyway would break next build outright, since webpack resolves dynamic imports statically — a worse failure than the one it avoids), and NOTHING MINTS A HANDSHAKE TOKEN. realtime-token.ts exists and is tested, services/realtime verifies it correctly, but no route in ROUTES returns one and no handler under src/app/api/** calls mintRealtimeToken. The browser has no way to obtain the credential the service requires. So the hook is the seam those two will plug into: a RealtimeTransport structural interface (NOT Socket from a package that is not installed — that would not compile) plus a fetchToken callback, both absent by default. When absent it constructs no transport, requests no token and schedules NO timer of any kind. Four tests assert that by checking the injected factory spy was never called, which is the only observable a hook cannot fake. serviceUrl is injectable because NEXT_PUBLIC_* is inlined at BUILD time and cannot be set from a test — the first version of these tests reported "unavailable" for the connected path for exactly that reason, and an untested connected path is one nobody notices is broken until a class is running. A fresh token is requested before EVERY reconnect: the TTL is 120 s, so a cached one yields "expired" from the middleware and turns a transient drop into a permanent one. Backoff is exponential with FULL JITTER, because a class ends and eighty browsers reconnect in lockstep otherwise.

  (5) JitsiEmbed — dispose() on every return path of the effect, including the failure paths. React removing the container div does NOT stop a Jitsi conference: the iframe, the window postMessage listener and the getUserMedia tracks all survive it, so the camera light stays on and the microphone keeps transmitting into a room the student thinks they left. That is the least acceptable defect available in this wave and it has its own test. The external script is loaded lazily INSIDE the effect and only when the flag is on — at module scope it would break SSR, pull ~200 kB from meet.jit.si onto every page in the LMS, and contact a third party for students who never open a class. The promise is shared at module scope so two mounts do not race two script tags. The leave POST is best-effort and the duration is a HINT: leaveClassSchema bounds it 0..600 and the handler clamps against wall clock, because it is a client-supplied number feeding a participation score.

  (6) The chat/Q&A transport arrangement is the INVERSE of the usual one: REST is primary, the socket only notifies. Reads always come from the REST history endpoint and writes always go through the Next route. Routing writes over a socket would mean persistence performed by a different process on a different host, so "did my message save?" would have two answers depending on which transport was up — and as built, the socket is a pure notification channel that can be absent without changing correctness. Optimistic send keys the pending row by a client ref, never by a negative id (a negative id flows into the delete route identically to a real one, and the first bug that produces is a student deleting message 1). Rollback KEEPS the text visible with a retry; silently deleting what someone typed is the worst possible response to a dropped request.

  (7) THE BRIEF'S "class_qa upvotes are unbounded, do not pretend one-vote-per-user is enforced" IS STALE AND I DID NOT BUILD TO IT. class_qa_votes(question_id, user_id) exists with the pair as PRIMARY KEY and the upvote route inserts with ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING inside a transaction, incrementing the denormalized counter only when the ledger actually inserted. The panel therefore locks the button after a vote and reads the server's authoritative total back. What it genuinely CANNOT know is whether the current user already voted on FIRST LOAD — the list endpoint returns the total, not the ledger — so a returning student sees "Upvote" until they press it once. Stated in the module header rather than hidden behind a hopeful hasVoted field that does not exist. The OTHER flagged defect, /api/classes/upcoming having no cohort scoping, IS still real: ClassCalendar surfaces it in the UI as a visible caveat rather than rendering a wrong list silently, and a test asserts the caveat is present.

  (8) Presentation builder: ALL mutation goes through slide-ops.ts, which ends every function with renumberSlides. PUT /slides/:slideNumber addresses a slide BY its 1-based number, so drifted numbering does not fail loudly — it silently overwrites the wrong slide. The editor saves the WHOLE document in one PUT for the same reason plus the one the route's own header gives about orphaned projection rows. Reorder is explicit up/down BUTTONS, not drag and drop: HTML5 drag events do not fire for keyboard users at all, and the accessible alternative is a grab/move/drop mode with live-region narration that this stream would be building from scratch with no primitive to lean on. Buttons work with mouse, touch, keyboard and switch on day one. Thumbnails compose the EXISTING RevealPresentation in readOnly mode — a second miniature renderer is a second place for a slide type to look different from how it will actually present.

  (9) Honest gaps in the components themselves: CodeSnippetViewer has NO SYNTAX HIGHLIGHTING, because no highlighter is a dependency and this stream may not add one (reaching into reveal.js's bundled highlight.js from outside a deck couples every code block in the LMS to a presentation library's file layout). ClassRecording renders a LINK, not a video element, for hlsUrl/dashUrl: neither plays without hls.js or dash.js outside Safari, so an embedded player would be a black rectangle for most students. ThemeSelector's theme list lives in the builder, not the contract, because theme.ts exports no enumeration — the theme route's own TODO says to validate against one when it exists; a select is the interim mitigation that stops a UI user typo'ing a theme that Reveal then silently ignores.

