[2026-08-01 14:10] defect remediation 1/3 — PUT /api/presentations/:id/slides/:slideNumber no longer silently edits the wrong slide.

  THE DEFECT, as the QA stream recorded it at item (7) of the 11:30 entry. The handler read the position from the URL segment only and overwrote the body's `slide.slideNumber` with it. A client that "moved" a slide by changing that field and PUTting to the OLD url rewrote the CONTENT of the slide at the old position and got a 200. Nothing anywhere said a thing: no error, no log, no differing status. The QA stream was right that "the URL is the address" is a defensible rule; the defect is not the rule, it is answering an ambiguous request with silence.

  THE DECISION: REFUSE WITH 409, DO NOT HONOUR THE BODY AS A MOVE, and the precedence rule the original author chose is left intact. A move is not a property of one slide. It renumbers every slide between the two positions and forces a rebuild of the deck's projection rows in the order `renumberSlides()` establishes — which is exactly why DELETE in this same file cannot be a single statement, and the header there already explains that `presentation_slides_number_idx` makes a naive 5->4 shift collide. Inferring that whole operation from a field every honest edit also sends would make "edit slide 4" and "move slide 4" indistinguishable on the wire, and would mean a client that forgot to update the field before saving reorders the deck by accident. A move wants its own endpoint with its own body; until one exists, refusing is the answer that cannot be wrong. 409 and not 422 because the body is well-formed — it conflicts with the resource it was addressed to, which is what 409 means. The e2e spec at tests/e2e/presentations/deck-workflow.spec.ts:236 already accepts 200, 409 or 422 for this case, so the change does not break it.

  The check is deliberately placed before `db.transaction`, so a mismatched request opens no transaction and reads no deck.

  REGRESSION TEST: src/app/api/presentations/[presentationId]/slides/[slideNumber]/route.test.ts, 3 tests. It asserts the 409 and the machine-readable `slide_number_mismatch` code, and asserts the message names BOTH numbers (a bare "conflict" does not tell the client which of the two it got wrong). The second test is the one that matters structurally: `@/db` is mocked as a Proxy that THROWS on any property access, so a handler that opened its transaction first and refused afterwards fails the test. A status-code-only assertion would have passed in that case and left the check free to drift after the write in a later edit.

