[2026-08-01 14:35] defect remediation 2/3 — a presentation regrade is now recorded, attributable and non-destructive. Security review Finding 2, mitigated rather than fixed, and the difference is stated.

  WHAT THE FINDING IS. SECURITY_REVIEW_ADDON_WAVE.md:160. POST /api/presentations/submissions/:id/grade scopes its UPDATE by submission id alone, and `apiGuard("instructor")` admits every instructor equally, so any instructor may overwrite any other's mark. Worse than the overwrite itself: `graded_by` was replaced along with the score, so the mark that was there before and the person who gave it were simply gone. Grades feed the leaderboard, which makes that a silent, unattributable change to a student's standing.

  WHAT WAS NOT DONE, AND WHY. Owner-scoping is not restored. It cannot be expressed: `assignments` has no owning-instructor column, so there is no column to AND into the WHERE the way classes/[classId]/end/route.ts does with `live_classes.instructor_id`. Adding one is a shared-contracts seam change with cross-stream consequences and was explicitly out of scope for this wave. The route's JSDoc continues to state the gap as a fact of the current model, and now also states that the history table is the only defence until that column exists.

  WHAT WAS DONE — option (a) of the brief, the append-only history, in preference to option (b)'s `supersedes` handshake. New table `presentation_grade_events` in src/db/schema.presentations.ts: submission_id, score, feedback, rubric_scores, graded_by, graded_at. One row per grading event INCLUDING THE FIRST — recording only supersessions would leave the first grade, the one a dispute is most often about, as the one grade with no row, and "no rows" would then be ambiguous between "never regraded" and "never graded". The grade handler now runs its UPDATE and this INSERT in ONE transaction, off ONE clock read, so a grade cannot take effect with no history row and the two rows' `graded_at` are equal rather than merely close.

  (b) WAS CONSIDERED AND REJECTED, not skipped. A required `supersedes` field carrying the current graded_by/graded_at would make an honest instructor correcting their own typo do a read-then-write round trip and would 409 them if a colleague graded in between; it would also break every existing caller of a route that is already shipped. And it prevents nothing an attacker cares about — an instructor who intends to overwrite simply reads the current grade first and sends it. It buys accident-prevention at the cost of the audit trail's simplicity; (a) buys the audit trail, which is what the finding actually asks for.

  ON `graded_by` BEING NULLABLE WITH ON DELETE SET NULL, a compromise named in the schema comment rather than left to be discovered. For an audit trail the ideal is that deleting a user cannot erase their authorship, but RESTRICT would make an audit row block an account deletion the LMS otherwise permits, and this table must never be the reason another operation fails. It mirrors presentation_submissions.graded_by. The event, its score and its timestamp survive a deletion; only the name does not — and there is a test asserting exactly that, so changing it to RESTRICT later is a deliberate act with a failing test in front of it.

  APPEND-ONLY IS ENFORCED BY THE ABSENCE OF AN UPDATE PATH, the same way schema.peer-review.ts §3 makes a submitted review immutable: no `updated_at`, no UPDATE or DELETE anywhere in src/ against the table, INSERT only in the handler. A rule or trigger would make it a database guarantee; this schema uses neither anywhere, so the invariant is written down instead of half-implemented.

  MIGRATION: src/db/migrations/0008_mighty_nebula.sql, GENERATED ONLY AND NOT APPLIED TO DATABASE_URL. Read in full: one CREATE TABLE, two ADD CONSTRAINT for the foreign keys, one CREATE INDEX, one CHECK. Purely additive — `grep -ci drop` over the file returns 0. It WAS applied to the local scratch database (localhost:5433/lms_qa) so the integration test below could run against real rows; that database is a throwaway and is not DATABASE_URL. drizzle.config.ts needed no change: the table lives in schema.presentations.ts, which is already listed.

  REGRESSION TESTS, DELIBERATELY SPLIT ACROSS TWO FILES BECAUSE NEITHER CAN SEE THE OTHER'S HALF. src/db/schema.presentations.grade-events.test.ts (4 tests, node environment, gated on TEST_DATABASE_URL and skipping loudly without it) proves the DATA property against a real Postgres: after instructor B regrades instructor A's submission, A's 82 and A's user id are still readable in order, the submission row still reports the current mark, the first grade is present too, the 0-100 CHECK fires with SQLSTATE 23514 AND constraint name `presentation_grade_events_score_in_range` (name asserted for the reason tests/integration/db/constraints.test.ts gives — an INSERT failing on a missing column looks identical to a pass), and the event outlives its grader's deleted account with graded_by NULL. src/app/api/presentations/submissions/[submissionId]/grade/route.test.ts (6 tests) proves the HANDLER property the database cannot see: that the row is written at all, inside the transaction and not beside it (the faked db's non-transactional `update`/`insert` throw), off one clock read, twice for two gradings with the two different graders, and NOT AT ALL on a 404 or a 422 — a history row for a submission that was never graded is a phantom grade in the audit trail.

  Verification for this commit: npx vitest run on the two new files — 10 tests, all pass, the database file run with TEST_DATABASE_URL against lms_qa. npx tsc --noEmit clean. npx eslint on the touched paths clean.


[2026-08-01 15:05] defect remediation 3/3 — a chat or Q&A message typed before React hydrates is no longer lost in silence.

  THE BUG, as the QA stream diagnosed it at item (6a) of the 11:30 entry and classified as a test trap. The server sends HTML containing a real textarea. Until the client bundle loads and React hydrates, that textarea accepts keystrokes like any other — into the DOM only, because React state does not exist yet. Hydration then renders the CONTROLLED textarea with its initial value of "" over the top. The keystrokes are gone, Send stays disabled because it is computed from the same empty state, and there is no page error, no POST in the server log and nothing anywhere saying anything was lost. It is a test trap AND a real bug: a student on a slow connection watching a class that has already started types the moment the field appears, which is the normal case rather than the edge one.

  THE FIX IS BOTH STANDARD REMEDIES, because each covers the other's gap, in src/components/live-classes/use-composer-hydration.ts. (1) ADOPT THE DOM VALUE. On mount — which is after hydration by definition — read the element's value and, if the student got ahead of React, lift it into state. When this works nothing is lost at all and the student never learns there was a race, which is why it is tried FIRST. (2) DISABLE UNTIL HYDRATED. (1) is not guaranteed: React may already have overwritten the DOM value during hydration, leaving nothing to adopt. So the composer is also disabled until the hook reports ready. Disabling alone would prevent the loss but would throw away the keystrokes in exactly the cases (1) saves, so it is the fallback, not the primary. Salvage runs BEFORE the enable, deliberately: enabling first would let a keystroke land in the gap and be overwritten by the value read a moment later.

  DISABLED WITH A STATED REASON, NEVER A BARE `disabled` ATTRIBUTE. The brief asked for this and the repo already required it — ChatPanel's own accessibility header says the composer is disabled WITH A STATED REASON when chat is off for the class, "never hidden, because a missing control is indistinguishable from a broken layout". The same argument applies with more force here: a dead field with no explanation reads as a broken page, and the student's response to a broken page is to reload, which loses the very draft the fix exists to protect. So the hook returns a sentence, not only a boolean, and both composers render it in the hint line and as the placeholder. The hydrating reason is ordered BELOW the permanent "chat is switched off" / "questions are switched off" message, which outranks a window that lasts a frame.

  APPLIED TO BOTH COMPOSERS: ChatPanel's message field and QAPanel's ask field. Not to QAPanel's per-question instructor answer boxes — those only exist after the question list has loaded, which is necessarily after hydration, so there is no window to protect. Nothing else in src/components/live-classes/ was touched; use-realtime.ts and its transport wiring belong to another stream running concurrently.

  REGRESSION TEST: src/components/live-classes/ChatPanel.hydration.test.tsx, 4 tests, in its OWN file rather than in ChatPanel.test.tsx — that file is another stream's, is being edited concurrently, and every test in it uses `render()`, which structurally cannot reproduce this bug. `render()` mounts on an empty container and so has no gap between "interactive HTML" and "React owns it", and the gap IS the bug. This file does the real sequence instead: renderToString, attach the HTML, set the textarea's value the way an impatient student does, and only then hydrateRoot. It asserts the server render ships the composer disabled AND carries the reason in the visible text; that the typed text survives hydration; that the composer and Send are both enabled afterwards, so the salvaged draft is actually sendable (a draft visible in the DOM but not in state would be the original bug wearing a different face); and that a read-only class still ends up disabled, so the pre-existing rule survives the new one.

  THE TEST WAS VERIFIED TO FAIL WITHOUT THE FIX, which for a silent bug is the only evidence worth having. Reverting ChatPanel.tsx alone and re-running gives 3 failures of 4, and the middle one is the defect in one line: `AssertionError: expected '' to be 'Can you go back a slide?'`.


[2026-08-01 20:15] realtime wiring: the live-class socket layer is connected. Three correct, tested, mutually unreachable pieces now form one path.

  WHAT WAS ACTUALLY WRONG. Nothing was broken; nothing was connected. services/realtime was a working Socket.io service with 150 passing tests. src/lib/live-classes/realtime-token.ts could mint and verify an HMAC-SHA256 handshake token and was security-reviewed clean. src/lib/live-classes/use-realtime.ts was a hook built explicitly as a seam. And mintRealtimeToken had ZERO call sites in src/, socket.io-client was not a dependency, and no route in the frozen ROUTES map returned a token — so the hook's gate (transportFactory !== undefined && fetchToken !== undefined) was false unconditionally and the whole feature ran on REST polling by construction. The hook's own header said all of this in full; this entry is the record of closing it.

  (A) THE DEPENDENCY, COMMITTED ALONE AND FIRST (f2344af). socket.io-client ^4.8.3 against the service's socket.io ^4.8.1 — same major, which is what matters: Engine.IO negotiates its protocol version per major and a mismatch surfaces as an opaque connect_error rather than a version complaint. Committed by itself because package.json is the most contended file in this wave and the window for a conflict should be one small commit wide.

  (B) THE MINTING ROUTE: POST /api/classes/:classId/realtime-token, "student" floor, liveClasses flag, registered in ROUTES and ROUTE_AUTH. ROLES_SATISFYING needed no edit — it is keyed by RouteAuth level, not by route, and "student" already exists.

  POST FOR A READ-SHAPED OPERATION, deliberately. Minting has no persistent effect, which normally argues for GET, but a GET is prefetched by browsers, followed by link scanners and cached by intermediaries, and a credential must be none of those. AUTHORIZATION IS A WHERE CLAUSE: id = :classId AND status IN (scheduled, active) — the same pair canJoin admits — so a class that has ended, was cancelled, or never existed produces no row, and "no row" is already the 404 path. 404 for all three, never 403, so the caller learns nothing about which ids exist. A test asserts the status literals are present in the predicate handed to the query builder, because "authorization" and "a forgotten if" look identical in a test that only checks the response.

  THE ROLE CLAIM IS A CAPABILITY IN THE ROOM, NOT A COPY OF THE USER'S ROLE, and this is the part worth reading twice. The socket service grants moderation — pinning, answering, deleting other people's messages — on role "instructor" alone. Copying the caller's database role would therefore let instructor B moderate instructor A's class from a token this route handed them. It mirrors mustOwn/ownershipFilter instead: admin is always "admin" (the documented cover-for-an-absent-colleague rule), an instructor is "instructor" only in a class they own, and everyone else — students, and instructors visiting a colleague's session — is "student". The ownership comparison is evaluated inside the SELECT that authorized the mint, so the elevated claim cannot drift from the row that justified it.

  MINTING IS RATE-LIMITED, precedent reused rather than reinvented: src/lib/execution/rate-limit.ts's SlidingWindowLimiter, a new policy (10 per 30 s, 60 per 10 min) in src/lib/live-classes/mint-limiter.ts. Bounded because the hook re-mints before EVERY connect and EVERY reconnect — the 120 s TTL makes a held token worthless — so a client with a reconnect bug is an unbounded stream of HMACs and row reads. The hook's own ceiling is six attempts, so a correct client never meets the limit. The limiter's stated weakness travels with it: a Map in one process, so on Vercel the effective ceiling is (instances x limit). Acceptable here because the socket service's per-user connection cap is the backstop that actually protects the room. THE LIMITER LIVES IN ITS OWN MODULE AND NOT IN THE ROUTE for a non-obvious reason that cost a build: Next.js type-checks route.ts against a closed export shape, and exporting a resetMintAllowance test helper beside POST fails the build with "Property resetMintAllowance is incompatible with index signature".

  No secret configured answers 503, not 500 and not a token — src/lib/features.ts calls an unprovisioned service a SUPPORTED state, and the secret is read BEFORE the query so an unprovisioned deployment does not pay a database round trip per retry.

  (C) THE TRANSPORT, src/lib/live-classes/socket-transport.ts. The adapter the hook's header predicted, plus the token fetcher.

  SSR CONTAINMENT, WHICH FORCED THE ONLY AWKWARD THING IN THE FILE. socket.io-client must never be evaluated on the server, and "use client" is not sufficient — client modules still execute during SSR. So the import is a dynamic import() behind a typeof window === "undefined" guard. But TransportFactory is SYNCHRONOUS — the hook calls it and immediately registers sixteen listeners — while import() is not, so the adapter returns a facade at once and QUEUES every on/off/emit until the socket exists. Making the factory async would have been tidier and was rejected: the seam is the contract, and the hook's connect/backoff/dispose logic is already written and tested against the synchronous shape. Queued emits are DROPPED on disposal rather than flushed, because replaying an emit onto a socket created after teardown is how an unmounted panel sends a message. Verified in the build output: no server page eagerly requires the socket chunk (it appears only as a lazy chunk and in .nft.json traces), and the client bundle carries it.

  SOCKET.IO'S OWN RECONNECTION IS TURNED OFF, and this is a correctness fix rather than a preference. Its retry REPLAYS THE ORIGINAL HANDSHAKE AUTH, and that token dies at 120 s, so every such retry after the window is guaranteed to fail "expired" — a transient disconnect becomes a permanent one. The hook re-mints per attempt and socket.io cannot, so the hook owns reconnection entirely and the adapter only reports. Two racing reconnect policies would also produce duplicate sockets that the service rejects as too_many_sockets.

  THE DEGRADED PATH IS UNCHANGED AND THAT WAS THE CONSTRAINT, not an afterthought. LiveClassRoom now defaults fetchToken/transportFactory to the real wiring, both module-level constants so their identity is stable and the connect effect does not churn. With no NEXT_PUBLIC_REALTIME_URL, isRealtimeAvailable() is false, the hook's gate is false, the factory is never called, no token is requested and no timer is scheduled. The four existing tests asserting exactly that are UNMODIFIED and still pass.

  (D) A REAL END-TO-END MESSAGE WAS PROVEN, which is the one claim in this file's recent history that could not previously be made about the socket path. The service was started locally on port 4111 with the in-memory store and a 40-character shared secret; a token was minted by the APP's own mintRealtimeToken with that secret; socket.io-client connected to /classes with the token in handshake.auth; the handshake was accepted, class:snapshot arrived, one chat:send was emitted and the authoritative chat:message came back in 6 MS (localhost, in-memory store, single client — a floor, not a production figure). The mint/verify pair, the namespace, the auth channel and two event names are therefore proven against the real service rather than against a mock.

  ONE FINDING FOR THE PANEL STREAM, discovered by getting it wrong: chatSendSchema is .strict(), so chat:send accepts EXACTLY { body, clientRef? }. The first attempt sent { classId, body } — reasonable-looking, since the wire types carry classId — and the server silently dropped it. No error, no ack, no log line at the client. Any panel that adds a field to an emit payload will fail the same silent way; the classId comes from the token, never from the payload.

  Verification: npx tsc --noEmit clean. npx eslint . clean, 0 warnings. npx vitest run — 183 files, 3457 tests, 3454 passed, 2 skipped, 1 failed; the failure is src/lib/problems/availability.test.ts:50, pre-existing on the base branch and untouched, and the pass count is up from 3421 by the 33 tests added here. npm run build PASSES. NOT VERIFIED: no Playwright run (the orchestrator holds port 3000); the browser adapter itself was never exercised against the real service — the 6 ms figure comes from socket.io-client driven from Node with the same options the adapter passes, not from the adapter's queue-and-flush code, which is covered only by its unit tests; no test connects a real browser to a real service through a real Next.js session, so the token route and the socket have been proven correct separately and never in one chain.

