[2026-08-01 07:55] feat(ui): root error.tsx, global-error.tsx and not-found.tsx — the app had ONE error boundary and no 404 page — why: audited for what takes the platform down in front of a student rather than in a test report, and this was the largest gap. Before these three files, src/app/(app)/practice/error.tsx was the ONLY error boundary in the entire application, so an unexpected throw anywhere else — a failed query on the dashboard, the leaderboard, a lecture page, the grading queue — fell through to Next.js's built-in error page: unstyled, unbranded, and with NO ROUTE BACK INTO THE APP. The audience here is students who cannot read a stack trace and cannot redeploy, so a dead end IS the failure. THE 404 IS THE MORE IMPORTANT OF THE THREE, and this is the non-obvious part: notFound() is the primary AUTHORIZATION outcome across several streams, not merely the answer to a mistyped URL — a peer-review allocation that is not the caller's, a coding problem below the caller's level, a locked week, an instructor route reached by a student. Those streams chose 404 over 403 deliberately so a probe cannot learn whether a row exists (argued in the peer-review headers). Every one of them was landing on the built-in page, which means the most common outcome of the platform's own authorization rules was a dead end. The new page therefore gives NO SPECIFIC REASON — "you do not have access to this submission" would hand back exactly the fact the 404 exists to withhold — and offers both /dashboard and /login, because it is reachable signed out. WHY BOTH error.tsx AND global-error.tsx, since one file looks sufficient: a segment error.tsx renders INSIDE the root layout and so cannot catch an error thrown BY that layout. global-error.tsx REPLACES the root layout, which is why it emits its own <html>/<body> and why its styles are INLINE — globals.css is imported by the very layout it is standing in for, so token class names would resolve to nothing there. That is deliberate, not an oversight, and is the one place in this repo where inline styles are correct. Neither renders error.message or a stack: production Next.js withholds those from the client because they carry file paths and query fragments, and re-adding them would undo that — `digest` is shown instead, which is the same value the server logs, so a student's screenshot is enough to find the entry. /practice keeps its own boundary (React uses the closest one) because it degrades the practice area alone and says so in its copy. No Suspense interaction: an error boundary does not buffer the response, so unlike a stray loading.tsx it cannot swallow a 404 status — verified, boundary-scope.test.ts and loading-shape.test.ts still pass (30/30 with cross-stream-contracts). typecheck and eslint clean. | feature/perf-and-subject-sections
[2026-08-01 08:30] fix(seed): demo users are RE-ASSERTED on every run, not skipped as "exists" (scripts/seed.ts) — why: the seed was NON-CONVERGENT for the one fact every e2e suite depends on. The demo-user loop was `if (!existing) { insert } else { note("exists") }`, so once an existing fixture account's password drifted, `npm run db:seed` reported "exists" and repaired NOTHING — permanently, across every subsequent run. THE INCIDENT: admin@codequeenshub.test (id=2) no longer matched DEMO_PASSWORD. Proven directly rather than inferred — a throwaway probe ran bcrypt.compare("Passw0rd!demo", passwordHash) for all three fixture accounts and got student=true, instructor=true, ADMIN=FALSE. THE COST, and this is why it is worth the long note: 26 failures spread across four suites whose test titles name no admin at all — activity-log (18 of 26 in that file), auth (4, including "the seeded demo accounts all log in"), analytics (2), certificates (2) — plus global-setup's warm-up timing out on the admin login with page.waitForURL: Timeout 120000ms while student and instructor warmed cleanly. That warm-up line was the only honest signal in the entire report and it was one line above 97 misleading ones. This is the SAME failure shape as the two environment faults logged earlier today: one piece of broken setup, reported as many feature bugs, in features nobody had edited. The seed's closing console.log PROMISES "Demo login password for all three accounts: Passw0rd!demo" — printing that while leaving a stale hash in place is asserting something the script has not checked, so re-hashing on every run is what makes its own output true. Role and cohortId are re-asserted for the same reason: a drifted role fails the authorization specs in the identically misleading way, and the seed is the DEFINITION of what these three accounts are. Scoped to the hard-coded DEMO_USERS trio of @codequeenshub.test addresses, so it cannot touch a real student's password — no real address is in that list. Verified: seed now reports "0 created, 3 updated, 46 already present" and the probe returns bcryptMatch=true for all three. A first attempt passed note("reset", …), which is not one of that function's three accepted labels — corrected to "updated", which is also what makes the summary line honest (it had printed "0 updated" while updating three rows). | feature/perf-and-subject-sections

[2026-08-01 09:40] fix(test): activity-log.spec.ts uses page.request, not the bare `request` fixture — 15 tests had NEVER RUN — why: `loginAs(page, role)` signs in by driving the form, so the session cookie lands in the PAGE's context. Playwright's top-level `request` fixture is a SEPARATE context with its own cookie jar, so every call through it was ANONYMOUS regardless of who the page had just signed in as. The rest of the suite already knew this — `page.request.*` appears 105 times across tests/e2e — and only this file used the bare fixture, in 22 places. Two failures were visible: "an instructor's API call gets 403, not 401" got 401 because the caller was nobody, and "there is no endpoint that accepts a hand-made log entry" got 401 against an expected [404, 405] (the guard answered before routing did, so the test could not see whether a POST handler exists at all). THE SILENT PART WAS WORSE: tableReady() used the same anonymous context, read 401, returned false, and the test.skip in three beforeEach hooks therefore fired on EVERY RUN — 15 tests, the whole query surface plus the entire export section, reporting "skipped" while never once executing. That is precisely the failure playwright.config.ts's header already argues about test.skip: it looks green while asserting nothing, "which is worse than a red run". Two call sites deliberately KEEP the bare fixture because they must not carry a session: the anonymous-401 check, and the cron endpoints, which accept only `Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET` and ignore cookies. One test improved by accident of the fix — "a browser session cannot trigger a prune, even an admin one" was passing while making an anonymous call, i.e. proving nothing about a browser session; it now sends the admin's cookies and still expects 401, which is the assertion the title claims. Also corrected an over-broad first pass of the rewrite that replaced `request.get` INSIDE tableReady's own body with `page.request.get`, where no `page` is in scope. | fix/production-hardening

[2026-08-01 09:40] DIAGNOSED, NOT FIXED: /settings shows no notice after a save, and shows a STALE ERROR after a later success — why recorded rather than patched: the cause is a Next.js 15.5.22 client-router behaviour, and the workaround attempted for it could not be verified, so it was reverted rather than shipped half-working. THE EVIDENCE, from a Playwright probe listening on `framenavigated` and on the POST response: the server does exactly the right thing, answering 303 with `x-action-redirect: /settings?profile=saved;push` verbatim. THE CLIENT ROUTER THEN DISCARDS THE SEARCH PARAMS — submitting from /settings lands on /settings, and submitting from /settings?profile=invalid_url lands on /settings?profile=invalid_url, i.e. the OLD query survives and the new one is ignored. Tested with both push and RedirectType.replace; identical. revalidatePath was ruled out by removing it and re-measuring: identical. A FULL NAVIGATION to /settings?profile=saved renders the banner correctly, which is what proves page.tsx, resolveNotice and FormNotice are all innocent — the mechanism was never reached, and account.spec.ts:129 was pointing at the save (which works: the probe confirmed the row is written and the values persist). USER IMPACT, and the second half is the worse half: (1) saving your profile gives no confirmation; (2) after one rejected save, a subsequent SUCCESSFUL save still shows "Check the links…", so a student is told their save failed when it succeeded. THE ATTEMPTED FIX, and where it stopped: a one-shot flash cookie set beside the query (query kept, because messages.ts's no-JavaScript argument still holds and the query DOES arrive when the browser follows the 303 itself), read by the page as a fallback, cleared in middleware because a Server Component may read cookies but not delete them. Two real bugs were found and fixed inside that attempt — `cookies().set` percent-encodes the value, so "profile:saved" returns as "profile%3Asaved" and an indexOf(":") parse silently yields nothing; and `await backWithNotice(...)` broke the type narrowing this file depends on, because TypeScript only treats a never-returning call as terminating in STATEMENT position, not inside an `await` expression (four errors, fixed with `return await`). After both, the banner still did not render. UNPROVEN HYPOTHESIS for whoever continues: Next renders the redirect target's RSC payload WITHIN the action request, where `cookies()` reflects the request rather than the value just staged on the response — if so, the flash cannot be read on the render that matters and the fix must not be a cookie. Recommended next step is to reproduce against a bare Next 15.5.22 app and, if confirmed, either upgrade or move the notice out of the URL entirely. | fix/production-hardening

[2026-08-01 11:20] fix(routing): each role now lands on and stays in its own view — role home, staff redirects, and a session-aware "/" — why: four bugs reported together, three of them the same cause. `ROLES_SATISFYING.student` is ["student","instructor","admin"], i.e. "student" means "any signed-in role", so EVERY student page admitted staff; and `AFTER_LOGIN_PATH` (src/lib/auth.ts) is one constant for all three roles. So an instructor or admin signing in landed on the STUDENT dashboard, and an admin opening /assignments got the student's list instead of /admin/assignments. Separately, "/" read no session at all: a signed-in user — including one who had just used SignOutButton, which lands there — was shown "Sign in" and "Create an account" with no route into their own area. NEW OWNER FOR THE ROLE->HOME QUESTION: src/lib/navigation/role-access.ts. ROLE_HOME is DERIVED from the first nav row of each role rather than restated, so it cannot drift from the sidebar, and src/app/(staff)/layout.tsx's inline `user.role === "admin" ? "/admin" : "/instructor"` now reads from it. THE FIRST DESIGN WAS WRONG AND IS WORTH RECORDING: derive the whole permission from NAV_LINKS — a staff role may open what its own sidebar offers. Tidier, and it breaks three things an inventory of the real routes turned up. (1) /assignments/ingest-status IS A STAFF PAGE living under the student /assignments prefix (its own guard is requireRole("instructor")), and two specs in tests/e2e/submissions navigate staff straight to it — a prefix redirect removes a working screen to fix a cosmetic one. (2) FORUM MODERATION IS SERVER ACTIONS POSTING TO /forums/* PAGE PATHS; there are no forum endpoints in the frozen ROUTES map, so removing an offensive post is a POST to the page itself and a redirect would discard it silently. (3) Staff deliberately USE several student-level pages and specs assert the staff experience on each: /leaderboard has a staff-only cohort picker, /courses returns a staff-bypass verdict with access-via=staff, /badges is required to degrade with a note rather than refuse because "a nav link whose destination refuses the person who can see it is worse than no link". So the redirect table is deliberately SMALL and enumerated — /dashboard, /assignments, /quizzes per staff role — and everything else is left alone. The rule that survived is narrower than "no role may see another's page": a staff member must not be shown a page that is ABOUT THEM AS A STUDENT, because they have no such data and the page is empty. GET ONLY in middleware, which is load-bearing rather than cautious, for reason (2) above; HEAD included because a HEAD is a document request. The query string is dropped on redirect, because a student page's filters mean something different against staff data. ROLES_SATISFYING IS NOT CHANGED: tightening it would break the staff features this change routes people TO — the grading queue, the live-class room an instructor is running, and the author-then-read-back pairs in the learning-content routes all read student-scoped endpoints on purpose. The distinction drawn is that staff must not RENDER a student's page, while staff code may READ student data through an endpoint that authorises them. The login action also picks its destination by role now, by email lookup rather than from the session — signIn(..., {redirect:false}) stages the cookie on the RESPONSE while auth() reads the REQUEST's cookies, so inside that one request the caller is still anonymous. An explicit `next` still wins, and "no next given" is no longer collapsed into "/dashboard" before the role is known, which is what the bug was. 79 new tests: 48 in role-access.test.ts (including the deliberate exceptions, a no-redirect-loop property, and a check that every target is a real nav href for that role), 20 in the first test src/middleware.ts has ever had (the GET-only rule asserted against POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE, plus a positive control so it cannot pass vacuously), and 11 for the landing page (every role's destination, an unknown role treated as signed out, and the page still rendering when the session read throws). | fix/role-based-routing

[2026-08-01 14:20] fix(env): stale .next build cache removed and dev server restarted; NO SOURCE FILE CHANGED - why: the reported "Runtime ChunkLoadError - Loading chunk app/(auth)/login/page failed" pointed a caret at src/app/(auth)/login/page.tsx:217, the <Link href="/register"> in the footer paragraph, which is why it reads as a code defect. It is not one. The overlay attributes a chunk-load failure to the source position of the component that was being rendered when the fetch failed, so the caret lands on whatever line the RSC payload was up to - it names a symptom's location, not a cause. Measured before touching anything: the running dev server was PID 22172 started 14:03, and Test-Path on .next/static/chunks/app/(auth)/login/page.js returned FALSE - the server was serving an HTML document whose script tag referenced a chunk that did not exist on disk. That is a .next cache that has drifted from the process holding it (the documented hazard of wiping or partially rebuilding .next underneath a live `next dev`), and no edit to page.tsx could have fixed it. ORDER MATTERS AND IS THE ONLY REASON THIS NEEDS A LOG ENTRY: the server was stopped FIRST, port 3000 confirmed free, and only THEN was .next deleted. Doing it the other way - deleting the cache under a live server - reproduces this exact ChunkLoadError and manufactures a fake defect in whichever page is opened next, which is how this state is most easily re-entered. Verified after restart: server Ready in 3.9s, GET /login = 200, and GET /_next/static/chunks/app/(auth)/login/page.js = 200, 158547 bytes - the chunk the error named now exists and is served. E2E: npx playwright test tests/e2e/auth/auth.spec.ts --project=chromium, 17 passed in 5.4m, covering register -> logout -> login, the three seeded demo accounts signing in, and the RBAC and sign-out groups. The one number worth carrying forward is the warm-up: "35 route(s) compiled in 237930 ms" on the cold cache, i.e. ~4 minutes of the run was rebuilding what was just deleted - a full .next wipe is the correct fix here but it is not a free one, so it should stay a deliberate step rather than a reflex. | feature/perf-and-subject-sections

2026-08-07T00:00:00Z | SUBMIT-SYNC-001 | src/lib/instructor/actions.ts | MODIFY | Added `syncSubmissionsAction` — a staff-guarded server action that runs `ingestAllAssignments({triggeredBy:"manual"})` then `persistMissedDeadlinePenalties()`, i.e. the cron sweep on demand. NOT a call to POST /api/cron/ingest-submissions: that endpoint accepts only `Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET` and additionally rejects any cookie-bearing request, so reaching it from a browser would have required putting the cron secret somewhere a button can see it — the exact confused-deputy leak that check exists to prevent. Guarded by `requireStaffAction()` (instructor OR admin) as the first statement, per the file's stated rule that every exported action is an HTTP-reachable endpoint. Returns a flat summary plus a per-assignment `problems` list built from `aborted`/`abortDetail`, because "0 submissions" and "the sheet is published as a web page instead of CSV" are indistinguishable from the grading queue and only one of them is the instructor's to fix. Revalidates the grading, analytics, assignments, dashboard and leaderboard paths. | feature/manual-submission-sync

2026-08-07T00:00:00Z | SUBMIT-SYNC-002 | src/components/instructor/SyncSubmissionsButton.tsx | CREATE | Client button for the action, following the ExportButton pattern (pending state + Toast). Reports the counters and the abort advice rather than succeeding silently; uses tone "info" not "success" when nothing was ingested, since a green tick on an empty run reads as "your submission is here". Calls router.refresh() so newly ingested rows appear without a reload. | feature/manual-submission-sync

